const BQ=[
{"id":"Q1","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"You are implementing the core agentic loop for your customer support agent. After sending each request to Claude, your harness code needs to decide whether to execute the tools Claude requested or present Claude's text response to the customer. During testing, you notice the loop sometimes terminates prematurely when Claude provides a status update to the customer mid-conversation while also requesting a tool call in the same response. Which signal should your harness inspect to make the continue-or-stop decision?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Check whether the response contains any text content - if it does, the agent is addressing the customer and the loop should stop"},{"l":"B","t":"Inspect the `stop_reason` field: continue executing tools when it is \"`tool_use`\" and terminate the loop when it is \"`end_turn`\""},{"l":"C","t":"Count the number of tool calls made so far and stop after a configurable maximum (e.g., 10 iterations)"},{"l":"D","t":"Parse the assistant's text for phrases like \"I have resolved\" or \"your issue is complete\" to detect when the agent considers itself done"}],"a":["B"],"r":"The `stop_reason` field is the designed, deterministic signal for loop control. \"`tool_use`\" means Claude wants tools executed; \"`end_turn`\" means it has finished. Responses can contain both text and `tool_use` blocks simultaneously, so stopping whenever text appears causes premature termination. Parsing the text for phrases such as \"I have resolved\" is a documented anti-pattern.","w":"A fails because responses legitimately contain text alongside tool calls; C uses an arbitrary cap as the primary mechanism; D relies on unreliable NL parsing.","x":["Incorrect. Responses can contain text and tool requests together, so stopping when text appears ends the loop too early.","Correct. stop_reason is the deterministic signal. Continue executing tools on tool_use and stop on end_turn.","Incorrect. A fixed maximum number of calls is an arbitrary cap and not the main control.","Incorrect. Parsing the assistant's wording for phrases like 'issue is complete' is unreliable."]},
{"id":"Q2","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Your support agent calls `lookup_order` to retrieve order details. Your harness code successfully executes the tool against your backend and receives the order data. A junior developer asks how the model actually sees this data so it can decide what to do next. What is the correct explanation?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The Claude API automatically executes tools registered in the system and incorporates results into the model's context - no additional code is needed"},{"l":"B","t":"Your code appends a `tool_result` block carrying the matching `tool_use` ID to the message history, then sends the updated conversation back to Claude"},{"l":"C","t":"The MCP server delivers the result through a callback registered when the tool was defined, so it appears in the model's context without extra code"},{"l":"D","t":"The harness writes the latest result into the tool's description, because the model re-reads every tool definition at the start of each turn"}],"a":["B"],"r":"The API is stateless - tool execution happens in your code, and results reach the model only by appending them to the conversation history and re-invoking the API. There is no automatic execution, no push mechanism, and tool descriptions are static definitions, not result containers.","w":"A and C describe automatic mechanisms that don't exist; D confuses static definitions with runtime data.","x":["Incorrect. The API does not execute tools. Execution happens in your own code, and the result reaches the model only when you send it back.","Correct. The API is stateless. Your code appends a tool_result block with the matching tool_use ID to the history and calls the API again, which is how the model sees the data.","Incorrect. There is no callback that places a result in the model's context. The harness has to send the result back.","Incorrect. A tool description is a static definition and is not a place to store runtime results."]},
{"id":"Q3","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Company policy caps autonomous refunds at $500. Refunds above this amount require human approval. During testing with prompt-based enforcement, you discover that in approximately 3% of cases the agent still processes refunds of $600-$800, particularly when the customer provides a compelling hardship story. The compliance team requires zero tolerance for policy violations with financial consequences. What is the correct enforcement design?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Move the $500 limit to the very top of the system prompt in bold, and repeat it as the last instruction - position emphasis will ensure compliance"},{"l":"B","t":"Add a hook that intercepts every `process_refund` call, blocks any amount over $500 and routes the case to the `escalate_to_human` workflow"},{"l":"C","t":"Add the $500 limit to the `process_refund` tool's description with the note \"CRITICAL: never exceed $500\" so the model reads it during tool selection"},{"l":"D","t":"Set the temperature to 0.0 so the model applies the refund rule deterministically and stops yielding to persuasive hardship stories"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Business rules with financial consequences require deterministic, programmatic enforcement. A hook intercepts the tool call before execution, making it impossible for a policy-violating refund to go through regardless of what the model decides. All prompt-based approaches remain probabilistic.","w":"A and C improve odds but retain a non-zero failure rate shown in testing; D misapplies a sampling parameter to a compliance problem.","x":["Incorrect. Bold text at the top and bottom of the prompt makes a violation less likely but still leaves the roughly 3% failure rate, which breaks a zero-tolerance requirement.","Correct. A hook intercepts every process_refund call before it runs, blocks amounts over $500 and routes the case to human approval, so a violating refund cannot go through whatever the model decides.","Incorrect. A warning in the tool description is still a prompt-level hint, so the model can ignore it and the failure rate stays above zero.","Incorrect. Temperature controls randomness in sampling. It does not turn a business rule into an enforced rule."]},
{"id":"Q4","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A customer writes: \"I need a refund for my damaged headphones from order #8834, I also need to update my shipping address to 123 Oak St, and I'm missing 500 loyalty points from my last purchase.\" Your agent currently handles only the first issue mentioned and waits for the customer to raise the others separately, resulting in multiple contacts for what should be a single interaction. Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution. How should the agent handle multi-concern messages?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Resolve the most urgent issue (the refund) and send a follow-up message asking if there's anything else - handling one concern well is better than three poorly"},{"l":"B","t":"Split the message into its three issues, investigate each against the shared customer context, then send one reply that resolves all three"},{"l":"C","t":"Escalate immediately, since a message with several concerns signals a frustrated customer who is better served by a human agent"},{"l":"D","t":"Ask the customer to open a separate ticket for each concern, so that every issue gets its own tracking and resolution history"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Multi-concern decomposition directly protects first-contact resolution: the agent splits the request, investigates each item against shared context, and responds once with a complete resolution. This is the designed pattern for handling compound requests.","w":"A silently drops two concerns; C escalates work within the agent's capability; D forces the customer to repeat effort across multiple tickets.","x":["Incorrect. Answering only the refund and asking if there is anything else drops the other two concerns and forces another contact, which hurts first-contact resolution.","Correct. Split the message into its three issues, investigate each against shared customer context, and send one reply that resolves all three. This is the pattern for compound requests.","Incorrect. Several concerns in one message do not mean the customer is frustrated, and the agent can resolve all of them, so escalation is unnecessary.","Incorrect. Asking the customer to open a separate ticket for each concern makes them repeat effort and creates the multiple contacts the team wants to avoid."]},
{"id":"Q5","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Your agent must verify the customer's identity via `get_customer` before any account operations. Production data shows that in 8% of cases, when customers volunteer an order number upfront (\"check my order #12345\"), the agent skips identity verification and calls `lookup_order` directly - occasionally leading to misidentified accounts. Prompt instructions stating \"always verify identity first\" have not eliminated this behavior. What change provides a reliable guarantee?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Add few-shot examples to the system prompt showing the agent always calling `get_customer` first, even when order numbers are provided"},{"l":"B","t":"Add a programmatic gate that blocks `lookup_order` and `process_refund` until `get_customer` has returned a verified customer ID"},{"l":"C","t":"Rename `get_customer` to verify_identity_REQUIRED so the tool name itself signals that it must be called before anything else"},{"l":"D","t":"Add a routing classifier that parses the user's message and pre-selects which tools are available based on the detected intent"}],"a":["B"],"r":"When a specific tool sequence is required for critical business logic, programmatic enforcement (a gate that blocks downstream tools until the prerequisite completes) provides a deterministic guarantee. Prompt-based approaches and naming conventions remain probabilistic.","w":"A is probabilistic and didn't solve the 8% failure rate; C hopes naming will change behavior; D addresses tool availability, not ordering.","x":["Incorrect. Few-shot examples improve the odds but remain probabilistic, and the prompt approach has already failed in 8% of cases.","Correct. A programmatic gate that blocks lookup_order and process_refund until get_customer has returned a verified customer ID gives a deterministic guarantee that verification happens first.","Incorrect. A tool name is a hint, not enforcement, so the agent can still skip the step.","Incorrect. A routing classifier changes which tools are available but does not enforce the order in which they must be called."]},
{"id":"Q6","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Your agent needs to normalize data from multiple MCP tools before reasoning about it. The `get_customer` tool returns timestamps as Unix epoch integers, `lookup_order` returns them as ISO 8601 strings, and `process_refund` returns status as numeric codes (1=pending, 2=approved, 3=rejected). Without normalization, the agent sometimes miscompares dates and misinterprets status codes. Which Agent SDK mechanism provides deterministic normalization?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Add a system prompt instruction telling the model to convert every timestamp and status code to a common format before reasoning"},{"l":"B","t":"A PostToolUse hook that converts timestamps to one format and status codes to labels before the model sees each tool result"},{"l":"C","t":"Create wrapper tools that call the original tools and normalize output - but this requires maintaining duplicate tool definitions"},{"l":"D","t":"Document each tool's timestamp and status formats in its description so the model can interpret every result correctly"}],"a":["B"],"r":"PostToolUse hooks intercept tool results and can deterministically transform heterogeneous data before the model ever sees it. The model then reasons about clean, consistent data without needing to perform mental format conversion.","w":"A relies on the model's unreliable mental arithmetic; C duplicates tool infrastructure; D informs but doesn't guarantee correct interpretation.","x":["Incorrect. Asking the model to convert formats in its head relies on unreliable mental conversion, so errors will continue.","Correct. A PostToolUse hook converts timestamps and status codes deterministically before the model sees each result, so it reasons over consistent data.","Incorrect. Wrapper tools can normalise output, but they mean maintaining duplicate tool definitions, which is more infrastructure than a hook.","Incorrect. Documenting formats in descriptions tells the model what to expect but does not guarantee it interprets every result correctly."]},
{"id":"Q9","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"The agent must escalate a complex billing dispute involving a partial refund, a shipping credit, and a loyalty point adjustment. The human agents receiving escalations do not have access to the AI agent's conversation transcript or tool call history. Currently, escalations include only the customer's name and a one-line summary like \"billing dispute - needs help.\" Human agents report spending 10+ minutes re-investigating cases the AI agent already analyzed. What should the escalation handoff include?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The raw JSON of every tool result gathered during the session, so the human agent has the complete, unfiltered data set"},{"l":"B","t":"A structured summary: customer ID, verified details, root cause found so far, amounts and order numbers, actions taken, next step"},{"l":"C","t":"A frustration score from 1 to 10 computed from the conversation, so human agents can prioritize the most upset customers first"},{"l":"D","t":"Only the customer's name and account number - human agents prefer to start their own investigation without bias from the AI's analysis"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Structured handoff protocols exist precisely because the receiving human lacks the transcript. The summary transfers the complete case state - identity, findings, figures, actions taken, and a recommendation - so the customer never has to repeat information and the human doesn't re-investigate.","w":"A dumps unprocessed noise requiring the human to parse JSON; C conveys mood not case state; D forces the human to redo work the AI already completed.","x":["Incorrect. Raw JSON from every tool result is unprocessed noise that the human would have to parse.","Correct. A structured summary with customer ID, verified details, root cause so far, amounts and order numbers, actions taken and the next step transfers the whole case state so nobody re-investigates.","Incorrect. A frustration score conveys mood and not the facts of the case.","Incorrect. Sending only a name and account number forces the human to redo work the agent already completed."]},
{"id":"Q14","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Your agent occasionally ends support sessions in an undefined state - the customer receives no resolution and no escalation to a human. This happens when the agentic loop terminates due to a `max_tokens` limit or an unexpected error, without the agent having reached a conclusion. Customers are left waiting with no follow-up. What orchestration-layer safeguard addresses this?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise `max_tokens` substantially so the agent always has enough room to reach a resolution or escalation before the loop stops"},{"l":"B","t":"Add a session guard that checks each session ends in a resolution or confirmed escalation, and escalates with its context if not"},{"l":"C","t":"Log every incomplete session and have the support team review the log weekly to follow up with the affected customers"},{"l":"D","t":"Add a 30-second timeout that closes stalled sessions and sends the customer a \"we're looking into it\" follow-up message automatically"}],"a":["B"],"r":"An orchestration-layer safeguard ensures every session terminates in a defined end state. If the loop exits for any reason without resolution or escalation, the guard catches this and forces escalation with accumulated context, preventing customers from falling into a void.","w":"A doesn't prevent all termination causes; C is reactive not preventive; D creates the same undefined state with a misleading message.","x":["Incorrect. Raising max_tokens does not prevent the loop ending for other reasons such as an unexpected error.","Correct. A session guard that checks every session ends in a resolution or confirmed escalation, and escalates with context if not, closes the gap whatever caused the exit.","Incorrect. Reviewing a log weekly is reactive, so customers wait a long time before anyone follows up.","Incorrect. A timeout that sends a 'we're looking into it' message leaves the same undefined state with a misleading message."]},
{"id":"Q26","s":2,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Yesterday you ran a long investigation into a memory leak in a session you named \"leak-hunt,\" identifying three suspicious allocation sites and narrowing the root cause to a connection pool. Today you want to continue exactly where that investigation left off, with all the findings and context from yesterday's session. What do you run?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"claude `--continue-all`, which reopens every recent session and lets you pick the one you want"},{"l":"B","t":"claude `--resume` leak-hunt, which reopens that named session with its full conversation context"},{"l":"C","t":"claude `-p` \"continue the memory leak investigation from yesterday\" - the `-p` flag with a descriptive prompt will reconstruct the context"},{"l":"D","t":"Start a new session and paste yesterday's terminal scrollback as the first message to provide the prior context"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Named session resumption (`--resume` <session-name>) is the designed mechanism for continuing specific prior work. It restores the full conversation context - all tool results, findings, and reasoning - so the investigation continues seamlessly.","w":"A is not a real flag; C starts a fresh non-interactive run with no memory; D approximates context badly with unstructured terminal output.","x":["Incorrect. --continue-all is not a real flag.","Correct. claude --resume leak-hunt reopens the named session with its full conversation context.","Incorrect. -p starts a fresh non-interactive run with no memory of yesterday.","Incorrect. Pasting terminal scrollback gives a poor approximation of the context."]},
{"id":"Q27","s":2,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"You completed a thorough analysis of a legacy module - reading 20+ files, tracing dependencies, and mapping the data flow. Now you want to compare two competing refactoring strategies: one extracts a service layer, the other introduces an event-driven pattern. You need to develop each approach independently from the same analysis baseline without cross-contamination. What capability fits?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Run both strategies sequentially in the same session, asking Claude to \"forget\" the first approach before starting the second"},{"l":"B","t":"Open two brand-new sessions and redo the complete module analysis in each before starting the respective strategies"},{"l":"C","t":"Use `/compact` between the two explorations to clear the first approach's context before starting the second"},{"l":"D","t":"Use `fork_session` to branch two independent sessions from the shared analysis, one strategy per branch"}],"a":["D"],"r":"`fork_session` creates divergent exploration branches that both inherit the completed analysis. Each branch evolves independently - the service-layer exploration doesn't influence the event-driven exploration. Clean comparison without re-paying the expensive analysis cost.","w":"A is impossible - conversation context cannot be selectively forgotten; B pays for the 20+ file analysis twice; C compresses context within one session but doesn't create parallel independent paths.","x":["Incorrect. Context cannot be selectively forgotten, so asking Claude to forget does not work.","Incorrect. Two new sessions would each repeat the expensive analysis.","Incorrect. /compact compresses context in one session but does not create parallel independent paths.","Correct. fork_session branches two independent sessions from the shared analysis, one strategy per branch, with no cross-contamination."]},
{"id":"Q30","s":3,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Your coordinator agent's system prompt clearly instructs it to delegate research tasks to specialized subagents. However, at runtime the coordinator attempts all research itself - calling web search tools directly, analyzing documents in its own context, and producing synthesis without any subagent involvement. Its `allowedTools` configuration restricts it to research-planning tools (`decompose_query`, `evaluate_coverage`, `compile_report`). What is the most likely cause of the delegation failure?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The coordinator runs on a model tier too small to follow multi-step delegation instructions reliably"},{"l":"B","t":"Subagents must be started and registered with the coordinator before it can hand any work to them"},{"l":"C","t":"The prompt should state \"DELEGATE\" in capitals, since emphasis is how coordinators learn priorities"},{"l":"D","t":"The coordinator's `allowedTools` omits \"Task\", the tool used to spawn subagents, so it cannot delegate"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Subagent spawning happens through the Task tool. A coordinator whose `allowedTools` omits \"Task\" has no mechanism to delegate - capability configuration takes precedence over prompt instructions, no matter how clearly written.","w":"A and C look for failures in comprehension when the issue is a configuration omission; B describes a startup model that doesn't exist - subagents are spawned on demand via Task.","x":["Incorrect. The behaviour is not a comprehension failure of a small model. Capability configuration is the cause.","Incorrect. Subagents are not started and registered in advance. They are spawned on demand through the Task tool.","Incorrect. Capital letters cannot give a coordinator a capability its configuration removes.","Correct. Subagents are spawned through the Task tool. The coordinator's allowedTools omits it, so it cannot delegate however clear the prompt is."]},
{"id":"Q31","s":3,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Your web search subagent gathers excellent, detailed findings about market trends. The synthesis subagent then produces a generic, surface-level report that references none of the specific data, statistics, or sources the web search found. The coordinator invokes the synthesis subagent with the prompt: \"Synthesize the research findings into a comprehensive report.\" What explains the synthesis agent's poor output?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The synthesis subagent needs extended thinking enabled to work through complex, multi-source research material"},{"l":"B","t":"Subagents don't inherit the coordinator's history, and the synthesis prompt never included the search findings"},{"l":"C","t":"The synthesis subagent needs its own web search tools so it can gather the findings it is meant to synthesize"},{"l":"D","t":"The word \"comprehensive\" in the prompt is too vague - replacing it with \"detailed\" would fix the output quality"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Subagent context isolation is a fundamental design principle: each subagent sees only what its prompt explicitly contains. \"The research findings\" refers to material the synthesis agent has never encountered. The coordinator must pass the actual data - search results, extracted facts, source URLs - directly in the synthesis prompt.","w":"A adds reasoning capacity over empty input; C duplicates the search agent's role; D adjusts wording for a prompt that's missing the essential data.","x":["Incorrect. Extended thinking adds reasoning capacity over input that contains no data, so it cannot recover the missing findings.","Correct. Subagents do not inherit the coordinator's history, and the synthesis prompt never included the search findings. The coordinator must pass the actual data in the prompt.","Incorrect. Giving the synthesis agent search tools duplicates the search agent's role instead of passing along the findings.","Incorrect. Changing 'comprehensive' to 'detailed' adjusts wording in a prompt that is missing the essential data."]},
{"id":"Q32","s":3,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Your coordinator sequentially invokes the web search subagent (15 seconds), waits for completion, then invokes the document analysis subagent (20 seconds) - resulting in 35 seconds total latency. The two research tasks are completely independent: web search looks at online sources while document analysis processes uploaded PDFs. How do you cut the latency roughly in half?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask each subagent to work faster by adding urgency instructions to their system prompts"},{"l":"B","t":"Have the coordinator emit both Task calls in one response so the two subagents run in parallel"},{"l":"C","t":"Merge the two subagents into a single agent that handles both web search and document analysis"},{"l":"D","t":"Run two separate copies of the entire coordinator system and merge their outputs in a downstream service"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Parallel subagent spawning is achieved by emitting multiple Task tool calls in one coordinator response rather than across separate sequential turns. Independent workstreams then execute concurrently, cutting latency to the duration of the slowest subagent rather than the sum.","w":"A adds urgency that doesn't affect execution time; C loses specialization for marginal gains; D duplicates the entire system for a one-line orchestration fix.","x":["Incorrect. Urgency in a system prompt does not change how long a subagent takes to run.","Correct. Emit both Task calls in one coordinator response so the independent subagents run in parallel, and latency becomes that of the slower one.","Incorrect. Merging the two into one agent loses specialisation for a marginal gain.","Incorrect. Running two copies of the coordinator and merging outputs duplicates the whole system for a one-line orchestration fix."]},
{"id":"Q33","s":3,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Your coordinator gives subagents rigid step-by-step research procedures: \"Run these five specific queries in this exact order, then summarize the top 3 results from each.\" When assigned a research topic that doesn't fit the scripted query patterns, subagents either produce irrelevant results or fail entirely. How should coordinator prompts be redesigned?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Make the procedures more detailed, with branching logic for each topic type the system is likely to receive"},{"l":"B","t":"Remove the instructions and let subagents infer their task from the topic and the conversation context alone"},{"l":"C","t":"Give subagents the research goal and quality criteria, and let them adapt their query strategy to what they find"},{"l":"D","t":"Have subagents pause and ask the human operator for guidance whenever the script does not fit the topic"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Goal-and-criteria prompts preserve subagent adaptability. The coordinator defines what success looks like (coverage depth, source quality, evidence requirements); the subagent determines how to achieve it. This is why you use an intelligent agent rather than a fixed script.","w":"A arms-races against topic variety; B removes the success criteria; D reintroduces a human into an autonomous loop.","x":["Incorrect. More detailed procedures with branching logic race against the variety of topics.","Incorrect. Removing the instructions also removes the success criteria.","Correct. Give subagents the research goal and quality criteria and let them adapt their query strategy to what they find.","Incorrect. Pausing to ask a human reintroduces a person into an autonomous loop."]},
{"id":"Q34","s":3,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Reports from your research system are coherent and well-written but consistently shallow on some subtopics - a 20-page report on \"AI in healthcare\" covers diagnostics thoroughly but barely mentions drug discovery, clinical trials, or regulatory implications. You want the system to notice and repair its own coverage gaps before finalizing. Which orchestration pattern achieves this?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Accept the first synthesis as final to control costs, and note the thinner subtopics in the report's introduction"},{"l":"B","t":"An iterative loop: the coordinator checks the synthesis for coverage gaps, re-delegates targeted queries, and re-synthesizes"},{"l":"C","t":"Ask the report subagent to expand thin sections from its own training knowledge so every subtopic gets balanced space"},{"l":"D","t":"Run every subagent twice on each report regardless of output quality, so the second pass catches what the first missed"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Iterative refinement closes the quality loop: evaluate coverage against criteria, dispatch targeted follow-up research for identified gaps, re-synthesize, and repeat until the report meets standards. This is adaptive - effort goes where gaps exist rather than being uniformly applied.","w":"A ships a known defect; C fills gaps with uncited generalities; D doubles cost blindly without gap-directed targeting.","x":["Incorrect. Accepting the first synthesis ships a known defect.","Correct. An iterative loop in which the coordinator checks the synthesis for coverage gaps, re-delegates targeted queries and re-synthesises closes the gaps.","Incorrect. Filling thin sections from training knowledge adds uncited generalities.","Incorrect. Running every subagent twice doubles cost without targeting the gaps."]},
{"id":"Q40","s":3,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Your coordinator agent always invokes all four subagents - web search, document analysis, synthesis, and report generation - for every query, including simple factual questions like \"When was the Paris Agreement signed?\" that only need a quick web search. This wastes resources and adds 30+ seconds of latency to simple queries. What should the coordinator do differently?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep running all four, since a consistent pipeline gives the most thorough answer whatever the query's complexity"},{"l":"B","t":"Assess what each query needs and invoke only those subagents: web search alone for simple facts, the full pipeline for research"},{"l":"C","t":"Remove three subagents and keep only web search, since simple factual questions make up most of the traffic the system sees today"},{"l":"D","t":"Let the user choose which subagents run for each query, since they know best how deep an answer they need"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Coordinators should dynamically select subagents based on query complexity. A simple factual question doesn't need document analysis, synthesis, or report generation. Dynamic routing optimizes both latency and cost while preserving full capability for complex queries.","w":"A wastes resources on simple queries; C removes capability for complex ones; D shifts architectural decisions to users who shouldn't need to understand the system's internals.","x":["Incorrect. Always running all four wastes resources on simple queries and adds more than 30 seconds of latency.","Correct. Assess what each query needs and invoke only those subagents: web search alone for simple facts, the full pipeline for research.","Incorrect. Removing three subagents removes capability for complex research queries.","Incorrect. Making users choose pushes architectural decisions onto them."]},
{"id":"Q42","s":3,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A coordinator agent delegates to a web search subagent and a document analysis subagent. During development, you discover the two subagents have begun exchanging messages directly - the web search agent sends URLs to the document analysis agent without going through the coordinator. This bypasses the coordinator's error handling and makes debugging difficult. What is the architectural risk?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"None, really: direct messages cut token usage and latency, so this is an improvement worth formalizing"},{"l":"B","t":"It breaks hub-and-spoke: the coordinator loses sight of that traffic, so error handling and logging become inconsistent"},{"l":"C","t":"Direct messages between subagents are unsupported by the API and will start failing with runtime errors"},{"l":"D","t":"The two subagents end up sharing one context window, which will exhaust the available context much sooner on long research tasks"}],"a":["B"],"r":"The recommended architecture is hub-and-spoke: the coordinator routes all inter-subagent communication. Direct communication bypasses the coordinator's oversight - error handling, logging, retry logic, and information routing all break down when agents talk without supervision.","w":"A ignores the operational risks; C may not cause errors but creates architectural problems; D misunderstands context isolation.","x":["Incorrect. Direct messages may save tokens, but they bypass the coordinator's oversight, which is an operational risk.","Correct. It breaks hub-and-spoke. The coordinator no longer sees that traffic, so error handling and logging become inconsistent.","Incorrect. The problem is architectural, not that the messages would produce runtime errors.","Incorrect. Subagents keep isolated context, so they do not end up sharing one window."]},
{"id":"Q51","s":4,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"You are asked to \"add comprehensive tests to a legacy codebase\" with no further specification. Nobody knows which modules are highest-risk, what the dependency structure looks like, or where the test coverage gaps are worst. Which decomposition approach fits this open-ended task?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Write tests file by file in alphabetical directory order, so coverage grows steadily and nothing is skipped by accident"},{"l":"B","t":"Generate the whole test suite in one large request, so the tests share consistent conventions across the entire codebase"},{"l":"C","t":"Map the codebase and its high-risk areas first, then write a prioritized test plan that adapts as you discover dependencies"},{"l":"D","t":"Test only the code modified in the last month first, since recent changes are statistically the most likely to contain new bugs"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Open-ended tasks with unknown scope require adaptive decomposition: understand the terrain first, prioritize by impact and risk, then let the plan evolve as each step reveals new information. A fixed sequence or an arbitrary filter ignores what makes the task open-ended.","w":"A spends effort by filename order rather than risk; B asks one pass to do what requires progressive discovery; D uses recency as a proxy for importance when legacy risk often lives in old, untouched code.","x":["Incorrect. Going file by file in alphabetical order spends effort by filename and not by risk.","Incorrect. One large request asks a single pass to do what needs progressive discovery.","Correct. Map the codebase and its high-risk areas first, then write a prioritised test plan that adapts as you discover dependencies.","Incorrect. Recency is a weak proxy for importance, and legacy risk often sits in old, untouched code."]},
{"id":"Q71","s":4,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"You are choosing decomposition strategies for two workflows: (1) a code review that always checks the same five aspects (security, error handling, naming, tests, documentation), and (2) a production incident investigation where each diagnostic step depends on what the previous finding reveals. Which pairing of decomposition patterns is correct?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Dynamic adaptive decomposition for both, since flexibility handles surprises in either workflow better than a fixed structure"},{"l":"B","t":"Prompt chaining for both, since fixed sequential steps are easier to test, debug and audit after an incident is closed"},{"l":"C","t":"Prompt chaining for the fixed five-aspect review; dynamic decomposition for the incident, where each finding sets the next step"},{"l":"D","t":"Dynamic decomposition for the review, so it can adapt to each change, and prompt chaining for the investigation, so it stays auditable"}],"a":["C"],"r":"The selection rule matches pattern to uncertainty: fixed sequential pipelines for predictable multi-aspect work, adaptive decomposition where the path emerges from intermediate findings. Each workflow gets the structure its uncertainty profile demands.","w":"A pays adaptivity overhead on a task with no uncertainty; B forces an investigation to follow pre-written steps that can't account for what each step reveals; D assigns each strategy to the workflow that defeats it.","x":["Incorrect. Adaptive decomposition for the fixed review pays flexibility overhead on a task with no uncertainty.","Incorrect. Fixed steps for the incident investigation cannot account for what each step reveals.","Correct. Use prompt chaining for the fixed five-aspect review and dynamic decomposition for the incident, where each finding sets the next step.","Incorrect. This gives each pattern to the workflow it does not suit."]},
{"id":"Q72","s":4,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"You are writing AgentDefinition configurations for your developer productivity system's subagents. A junior engineer claims subagents automatically inherit the parent coordinator's conversation history and share memory between invocations. Which statement correctly sets out both corrections?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Subagents inherit the coordinator's conversation history, but tool results are stripped before the subagent starts"},{"l":"B","t":"Each subagent has its own AgentDefinition (description, prompt, tools) and no shared memory, so pass needed context explicitly"},{"l":"C","t":"Subagents share memory through a global context store that the Task tool manages across every invocation of the same agent"},{"l":"D","t":"Subagents inherit history only when spawned with `inheritContext`: true, which is off by default for safety and cost reasons"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Two fundamental facts about subagents: they are configured individually (own description, system prompt, tool restrictions), and they operate with complete isolation - no inherited history, no shared memory across invocations. Context passing is always explicit.","w":"A, C, and D describe inheritance and sharing mechanisms that don't exist. The isolation model is by design, not a limitation to work around.","x":["Incorrect. Subagents do not inherit the coordinator's history.","Correct. Each subagent has its own AgentDefinition (description, prompt, tools) and no shared memory, so pass the context it needs explicitly.","Incorrect. There is no global context store that shares memory across invocations.","Incorrect. There is no inheritContext setting. Subagents are isolated by design."]},
{"id":"Q81","s":2,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"You want to resume last week's refactoring session, but since then the team has merged substantial changes across 15 of the 20 files the session analyzed. Most of the session's tool results now describe code that no longer exists. What is the reliable approach?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Resume the session as it is, since Claude detects which files changed and re-reads them before relying on old results"},{"l":"B","t":"Start a new session seeded with a summary of the prior conclusions and decisions, since its tool results are now stale"},{"l":"C","t":"Resume the session and raise `max_tokens`, giving the model room to reconcile the stale results with the current files"},{"l":"D","t":"Start completely from scratch and redo the analysis, since a session older than a few days should never be resumed in any form"}],"a":["B"],"r":"The resumption tradeoff: resume when prior context is mostly valid; start fresh with an injected summary when tool results have gone stale. With 75% of files changed, the prior tool results are more misleading than helpful - keep the conclusions, discard the outdated evidence.","w":"A assumes automatic re-verification that doesn't happen; C spends tokens on a correctness problem; D throws away a valid technique for cases where files haven't changed.","x":["Incorrect. Claude does not automatically re-read changed files before relying on old results.","Correct. Start a new session seeded with a summary of the prior conclusions and decisions, because most of its tool results are stale.","Incorrect. A larger max_tokens spends tokens on a correctness problem and does not fix stale results.","Incorrect. Never resuming old sessions throws away a valid technique for cases where files have not changed."]},
{"id":"Q82","s":3,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Your research coordinator's task decomposition is too narrow: asked to research 'impact of AI on creative industries,' it decomposed the topic into only 'AI in digital art,' 'AI in graphic design,' and 'AI in photography' - completely missing music, writing, film, and game development. The subagents executed their narrow assignments correctly. What is the root cause?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The subagents need larger context windows so each one can cover more creative domains within its single assignment"},{"l":"B","t":"The coordinator's decomposition is too narrow, so the subagent assignments never covered all the relevant domains"},{"l":"C","t":"The web search subagent's queries were not broad enough to surface music, writing, film and games on its own initiative"},{"l":"D","t":"The synthesis agent should have noticed the coverage gaps and filled them from its own general knowledge"}],"a":["B"],"r":"The coordinator's logs reveal the root cause directly: it decomposed 'creative industries' into only visual arts subtasks, completely omitting music, writing, film, and games. The subagents worked correctly within their assigned scope - the problem is what they were assigned, not how they executed.","w":"A, C, and D incorrectly blame downstream agents that performed their narrow assignments correctly. The decomposition is where coverage is determined.","x":["Incorrect. The subagents did their narrow assignments correctly, so their context size is not the problem.","Correct. The coordinator decomposed the topic too narrowly, so the subagent assignments never covered the relevant domains.","Incorrect. The search subagent searched what it was assigned, so its queries are not the cause.","Incorrect. Coverage is decided at decomposition, and the synthesis agent should not have to fill gaps from general knowledge."]},
{"id":"Q86","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A customer asks about a policy that doesn't exist in your knowledge base - they want to know if your company matches competitor prices. Your return policy only covers price adjustments for items purchased on your own website. The agent cannot find any applicable policy. What should the agent do?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Apply the own-site price adjustment policy, since it is the closest documented match to competitor price matching"},{"l":"B","t":"Tell the customer the company has no price matching policy and close the case, since nothing in the knowledge base covers it"},{"l":"C","t":"Escalate to a human agent, since policy is silent on competitor price matching and the agent must not improvise one"},{"l":"D","t":"Offer a 10% goodwill discount to resolve the request quickly without having to stretch any existing policy to fit"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Policy gaps - where the policy is silent or ambiguous on the customer's specific situation - are a legitimate escalation trigger. The agent should not improvise policy it's not authorized to create, nor should it dismiss the customer when a human might have the authority to help.","w":"A misapplies a different policy; B may be wrong if a policy exists that the agent hasn't found; D exceeds the agent's authorization to create ad-hoc discounts.","x":["Incorrect. Applying the own-site policy to competitor price matching misapplies a different policy.","Incorrect. Closing the case with 'no policy' may be wrong if a policy exists that the agent has not found, and it dismisses the customer.","Correct. Escalate to a human, because policy is silent on competitor price matching and the agent must not improvise one.","Incorrect. A goodwill discount exceeds the agent's authority."]},
{"id":"Q99","s":3,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Your coordinator needs to partition a broad research topic across subagents to minimize duplication. It assigns 'renewable energy policy in Europe' to one subagent and 'electric vehicle adoption in Europe' to another. However, findings about EV subsidies (which are both energy policy and EV adoption) are missed entirely because neither subagent claims the intersection. How should scope assignment be improved?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Give each subagent even narrower, strictly non-overlapping scopes, so it is always clear which agent owns each finding"},{"l":"B","t":"Let every subagent research the full topic independently and deduplicate the combined findings at the synthesis stage"},{"l":"C","t":"Assign deliberate overlap at known intersections and deduplicate at synthesis, since covering a topic twice beats missing it"},{"l":"D","t":"Add a third subagent dedicated to intersection topics, so overlapping areas such as EV subsidies have one clear, accountable owner"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Some controlled overlap between subagent scopes ensures intersections are covered. The synthesis step can deduplicate overlapping findings, but it cannot recover topics that no subagent investigated. Missing an intersection is worse than covering it twice.","w":"A makes the gap worse with stricter boundaries; B fully duplicates all work; D creates a fragile architecture where intersection detection becomes its own complex problem.","x":["Incorrect. Narrower, strictly non-overlapping scopes make the gap at the intersection worse.","Incorrect. Having every subagent research the full topic duplicates all the work.","Correct. Assign deliberate overlap at known intersections and deduplicate at synthesis, because covering a topic twice beats missing it.","Incorrect. A third subagent for intersections creates a fragile design where detecting intersections becomes its own problem."]},
{"id":"Q7","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Your `process_refund` tool currently returns the string \"Operation failed\" for every type of failure - whether it's a temporary database timeout, an invalid order number, or a business policy rejection. In production, the agent responds by retrying policy-blocked refunds (which will never succeed) and giving up on temporary outages (which would succeed on retry). What should the error response include to enable correct recovery behavior?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A longer error message with more detail in natural language, like \"The refund could not be processed due to a system issue or policy violation\""},{"l":"B","t":"Structured fields: an `errorCategory` (transient, validation, business or permission), an `isRetryable` flag and a short description"},{"l":"C","t":"An HTTP-style status code such as 503 for timeouts and 403 for policy blocks, so the agent can follow familiar web conventions"},{"l":"D","t":"A success response carrying a partial-completion flag, so the conversation stays positive while the agent decides what to do next"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Structured error metadata gives the agent the information it needs to make appropriate recovery decisions: retry transient errors, explain business rejections to the customer, and stop wasting attempts on non-retryable failures.","w":"A provides more detail but no machine-parseable signal; C uses web conventions the model may not reliably interpret; D suppresses failures, creating misinformation.","x":["Incorrect. A longer natural-language message adds detail but gives no machine-readable signal about whether to retry.","Correct. Structured fields such as errorCategory, isRetryable and a short description let the agent retry transient errors, explain business rejections and stop retrying what can never succeed.","Incorrect. Web status codes are less explicit than a retryable flag, and the model may not interpret them reliably.","Incorrect. A success response that hides a failure creates misinformation and stops the agent from handling the failure."]},
{"id":"Q8","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Production logs show your agent frequently confuses `get_customer` and `lookup_order`. Both tools currently have one-line descriptions: \"Retrieves customer information\" and \"Retrieves order details.\" They accept similar identifier formats (both can take an email or a numeric ID). When a customer says \"check my order #12345,\" the agent calls `get_customer` 40% of the time instead of `lookup_order`. What is the most effective first step to fix this misrouting?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Consolidate both tools into a single `lookup_entity` tool that accepts any identifier and internally determines which backend to query"},{"l":"B","t":"Add 5-8 few-shot examples to the system prompt demonstrating correct tool selection patterns for various query types"},{"l":"C","t":"Rewrite both descriptions to state the inputs each accepts, example queries, and when to use it rather than the other tool"},{"l":"D","t":"Add a keyword-based routing layer that picks the right tool from the request text before the model ever sees the request"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Tool descriptions are the primary mechanism LLMs use for tool selection. Expanding them with input formats, example queries, and explicit boundary language (e.g., \"use `lookup_order` when an order number is present\") directly addresses the root cause of misrouting.","w":"A is a valid architectural choice but more effort than a first step warrants; B adds token overhead without fixing descriptions; D bypasses the model's natural language understanding.","x":["Incorrect. Merging both tools into one that works out the backend internally is a valid architectural choice but much more work than a first fix.","Incorrect. Few-shot examples add prompt tokens without fixing the vague tool descriptions that cause the confusion.","Correct. Descriptions are the main way a model chooses tools. State the accepted inputs, example queries and when to use each tool instead of the other.","Incorrect. A keyword router replaces the model's language understanding instead of fixing the descriptions."]},
{"id":"Q35","s":3,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Every subagent in your research system currently receives the full 18-tool catalog. Production logs reveal the synthesis agent attempting web searches (and failing), the web search agent trying to parse PDF documents (and producing garbage), and frequent wrong-tool selections across all subagents. What is the correct architectural fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Add a 19th meta-tool that tells each agent which of the 18 tools suits its current task, so selection stays centralized"},{"l":"B","t":"Alphabetize and group the tool list so each agent can find the right tool more easily among the 18"},{"l":"C","t":"Write longer system prompts warning each agent about the 14 or more tools it should not use for its role"},{"l":"D","t":"Scope each subagent's tools to its role: search tools for the search agent, parsing tools for the analyzer, and so on"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Tool distribution is an architectural control. Agents choose more reliably among 4-5 role-relevant tools than among 18 unrelated ones. Out-of-role tools aren't just unused - they're actively misused because they're available.","w":"A adds selection complexity to solve selection complexity; B changes presentation not decision difficulty; C asks prompts to fight a problem that configuration should solve.","x":["Incorrect. A meta-tool that recommends tools adds selection complexity to solve selection complexity.","Incorrect. Alphabetising and grouping the list changes presentation, not how hard each decision is.","Incorrect. Long prompts warning agents about the tools they should not use ask a prompt to solve a problem that configuration should solve.","Correct. Scope each subagent's tools to its role. Agents choose more reliably among a few relevant tools, and out-of-role tools get misused because they are available."]},
{"id":"Q36","s":3,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Subagents in your research pipeline burn 30-40% of their tool call budget on exploratory calls before doing actual research - listing available document collections, probing for database schemas, checking what issue categories exist. This discovery overhead significantly increases latency and cost. Which MCP capability reduces this?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise each subagent's tool call budget so the discovery phase no longer crowds out the actual research"},{"l":"B","t":"Write the current data inventory into each subagent's system prompt so it knows what exists from the start"},{"l":"C","t":"Expose the catalogs, such as document hierarchies and schema listings, as MCP resources agents can read up front"},{"l":"D","t":"Remove the discovery tools so agents go straight to research instead of spending calls on exploration"}],"a":["C"],"r":"MCP resources are designed for exactly this: exposing browsable content catalogs that give agents visibility into available data upfront. Discovery becomes a resource read instead of dozens of exploratory tool calls.","w":"A pays for inefficiency instead of eliminating it; B goes stale the moment data changes; D replaces wasted calls with blind guessing.","x":["Incorrect. A larger budget pays for the inefficiency instead of removing it.","Incorrect. A data inventory written into the system prompt goes stale as soon as the data changes.","Correct. MCP resources expose browsable catalogues such as document hierarchies and schema listings, so discovery becomes a read instead of many exploratory tool calls.","Incorrect. Removing the discovery tools replaces wasted calls with blind guessing."]},
{"id":"Q44","s":4,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Your developer productivity agent must accomplish two tasks: (a) locate every file matching the test naming convention (`*.test.tsx`) anywhere in the repository, and (b) find every location where the string \"PaymentDeclinedError\" appears in code. A junior developer suggests using Grep for both tasks. Why is this incorrect, and what is the right tool pairing?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Glob with **/`*.test.tsx` to find the test files by path, and Grep to search file contents for \"PaymentDeclinedError\""},{"l":"B","t":"Grep with a filename filter to find the test files, and Glob with a content pattern to find the error string"},{"l":"C","t":"Bash with find and cat piped together for both tasks - the built-in tools are wrappers around these anyway"},{"l":"D","t":"Read every file in the repository and search the contents manually for both the naming pattern and the error string"}],"a":["A"],"r":"The tool division is clean: Glob matches file paths against patterns (names, extensions, directories); Grep searches file contents for text patterns (identifiers, error strings, imports). Using the right tool for each task is more efficient and more reliable than misapplying one tool to both.","w":"B swaps the tools into each other's territory; C abandons purpose-built tools for brute-force; D exhausts the context window immediately.","x":["Correct. Glob matches file paths such as **/*.test.tsx, and Grep searches file contents for text such as PaymentDeclinedError.","Incorrect. This swaps the tools into each other's territory. Grep does not match file paths, and Glob does not search contents.","Incorrect. Piping find and cat abandons the purpose-built tools for brute force.","Incorrect. Reading every file would exhaust the context window straight away."]},
{"id":"Q45","s":4,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Your agent attempts to use the Edit tool to modify a legacy file, but the edit fails: the anchor text it targeted (a common boilerplate pattern like \"if (error) { return null; }\") appears in six places in the file. The Edit tool requires a unique text match and rejects ambiguous anchors. What is the standard fallback approach?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Remove five of the six duplicate occurrences so that the one you need to change becomes a unique match"},{"l":"B","t":"Retry the same Edit call a few times, since the tool may resolve the anchor on a later attempt"},{"l":"C","t":"Switch to Bash and use sed with line-number targeting to make the change at the exact location"},{"l":"D","t":"Read the whole file, make the change in the full text, then Write the complete modified file back"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Read + Write is the documented fallback for Edit's unique-match requirement. Loading the full file gives you complete control over the modification without depending on text uniqueness. This is reliable regardless of how repetitive the file's structure is.","w":"A mutilates code to satisfy a tool requirement; B retries a deterministic failure; C uses fragile stream editing when a purpose-built fallback exists.","x":["Incorrect. Deleting five of the six occurrences changes the code to satisfy a tool.","Incorrect. Retrying a deterministic failure will not change the result.","Incorrect. sed with line numbers is a fragile workaround when there is a purpose-built fallback.","Correct. Read the whole file, make the change in the full text, then Write the complete file back. It does not depend on text being unique."]},
{"id":"Q46","s":4,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A developer asks the agent to explain how the billing module works. The agent immediately begins reading every file in the src/billing/ directory (30+ files) and exhausts its context window before producing any answer. Most of the files turn out to be helper utilities and type definitions irrelevant to understanding the core billing flow. What is the correct exploration strategy?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Read files in alphabetical order until the context budget is spent, then answer with whatever was loaded"},{"l":"B","t":"Grep for entry points such as exported functions and key class names, then Read selectively along the core import chains"},{"l":"C","t":"Ask the developer to describe the billing module first, then read only the files needed to confirm that their explanation is right"},{"l":"D","t":"Read the `README.md` in the billing directory and answer from the documentation, which summarizes the design"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Systematic incremental exploration: use Grep to locate relevant entry points (function names, imports, error patterns), then Read selectively to trace the flows that matter. This builds targeted understanding while managing the context budget - spending tokens on relevant code rather than exhaustively loading everything.","w":"A wastes context on irrelevant files by alphabetical accident; C outsources the agent's core job; D trusts documentation that may be outdated or missing in legacy code.","x":["Incorrect. Reading files in alphabetical order spends the context on whatever comes first, mostly irrelevant helpers.","Correct. Grep for entry points such as exported functions and key class names, then Read selectively along the core import chains. Tokens go to relevant code.","Incorrect. Asking the developer to describe the module hands the agent's core job to the user.","Incorrect. Answering only from the README trusts documentation that may be outdated or missing."]},
{"id":"Q47","s":4,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Your team shares a GitHub MCP server that requires a personal access token for authentication. You also run an experimental MCP server for a side project that you don't want to appear in teammates' environments. How should these two servers be configured?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Both servers in the project's `.mcp.json` file, with the GitHub token pasted directly so teammates don't need to configure anything"},{"l":"B","t":"GitHub server in project `.mcp.json` with ${GITHUB_TOKEN} expansion; the experimental one in your user-scoped ~/`.claude.json`"},{"l":"C","t":"Both servers in each developer's ~/`.claude.json`, with setup steps and the token location documented in the team wiki"},{"l":"D","t":"The shared server in `CLAUDE.md` as a configuration instruction and the personal one in `.claude/commands/`"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Scoping follows audience: `.mcp.json` (project-scoped, version-controlled) distributes team tooling to all developers, with ${VAR} expansion keeping credentials out of the repo. ~/`.claude.json` (user-scoped) holds personal servers that stay private.","w":"A commits a live credential to version control; C makes shared infrastructure a manual per-developer chore; D puts server config in files that don't configure servers.","x":["Incorrect. Pasting a live token into the committed .mcp.json puts a credential in version control.","Correct. Put the shared GitHub server in the project .mcp.json with ${GITHUB_TOKEN} expansion, and the experimental server in your user-scoped ~/.claude.json.","Incorrect. Putting both servers in each developer's own file makes shared infrastructure a manual chore for every developer.","Incorrect. CLAUDE.md and the commands folder do not configure MCP servers."]},
{"id":"Q48","s":4,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Your agent consistently prefers the built-in Grep tool over a custom MCP tool that provides semantic code search - understanding function relationships, type hierarchies, and call graphs beyond simple text matching. When asked to \"find all code affected by changing the User interface,\" the agent runs Grep for the string \"User\" and returns hundreds of irrelevant matches instead of using the semantic search tool that would return precisely the affected code. What is the likely cause and fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The MCP tool is failing silently and returning no results, so the agent falls back to the built-in Grep tool"},{"l":"B","t":"The MCP tool's description doesn't explain its semantic capabilities; describe how it differs from text-based Grep"},{"l":"C","t":"Claude Code ranks built-in tools above MCP tools by design, so this preference is expected behavior and cannot be tuned by the team"},{"l":"D","t":"The agent needs a larger context window so it can compare both tools' outputs before deciding which to trust"}],"a":["B"],"r":"When a model consistently favors a built-in tool over a more capable MCP tool, the MCP tool's description usually doesn't make its advantage clear. Enhancing the description with specific capabilities, use-case examples, and explicit differentiation from Grep shifts the model's selection behavior.","w":"A assumes a technical failure when it's a description quality issue; C is false - MCP tools compete on description quality; D addresses post-hoc evaluation, not selection.","x":["Incorrect. Nothing suggests the MCP tool is failing silently. The agent never chose it.","Correct. The MCP tool's description does not explain its semantic capabilities. Describe how it differs from text-based Grep so the model picks it.","Incorrect. MCP tools compete on description quality, so this is not a fixed built-in preference.","Incorrect. A larger context window does not change which tool the model selects."]},
{"id":"Q67","s":6,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A single `analyze_document` MCP tool handles three distinct operations - extraction, summarization, and claim verification - selected by a mode parameter. The agent frequently selects the wrong mode: using \"extract\" when it should verify, and \"summarize\" when it should extract. Results are inconsistent and debugging is difficult because the tool's behavior depends on a parameter the model struggles to select correctly. What is the recommended redesign?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Add a fourth \"auto\" mode that detects the right operation internally, so the model no longer has to pick a mode at all"},{"l":"B","t":"Write a much longer description that explains each mode in detail, with worked examples of when each one should be selected by the agent"},{"l":"C","t":"Merge extraction and summarization into one mode, leaving two modes so the choice the model makes is far simpler"},{"l":"D","t":"Split it into three tools (`extract_data_points`, `summarize_content`, `verify_claim_against_source`), each with its own schema"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Purpose-specific tools turn a hidden mode decision into a visible tool selection decision - the thing tool descriptions are designed to guide. Each tool's contract is explicit, input/output expectations are clear, and misrouting drops because the model selects based on clear descriptions rather than opaque mode parameters.","w":"A buries the selection problem one layer deeper; B improves docs of an inherently ambiguous structure; C keeps the overloaded design with fewer options.","x":["Incorrect. An 'auto' mode buries the selection problem one layer deeper.","Incorrect. A longer description improves the documentation of an inherently ambiguous structure.","Incorrect. Merging two modes keeps the overloaded design with fewer options.","Correct. Split it into three purpose-specific tools with their own schemas. A hidden mode choice becomes a visible tool selection, which descriptions can guide."]},
{"id":"Q68","s":6,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Your MCP extraction tool encounters a parsing failure when processing a corrupted PDF. It returns the message \"Error: could not parse document\" as an ordinary successful text result (`isError` not set). The agent then treats that error message as the document's content and attempts to \"extract\" fields from the sentence \"Error: could not parse document,\" producing nonsensical structured output with the vendor name \"Error\" and the amount \"could not.\" What is the correct MCP error handling pattern?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Prefix error messages with \"SYSTEM ERROR:\" so the agent recognizes them as errors through text conventions"},{"l":"B","t":"Return the failure with the MCP `isError` flag set, so the agent recognizes it as a tool failure, not content"},{"l":"C","t":"Return an empty string on failure, so the agent sees there is no content and does not try to extract any fields from it"},{"l":"D","t":"Throw an unhandled exception so the MCP connection drops and the client retries the call automatically with a fresh session"}],"a":["B"],"r":"The MCP `isError` flag is the designed channel for communicating tool failures. When set, the agent recognizes the response as an error condition and can reason about recovery - retry, skip, or escalate. Without it, error text is indistinguishable from legitimate content.","w":"A relies on the model inferring failure from prose conventions; C replaces a mislabeled failure with a silent one; D turns a recoverable tool error into a transport-level breakdown.","x":["Incorrect. A 'SYSTEM ERROR:' prefix relies on the model inferring failure from a text convention.","Correct. Return the failure with the MCP isError flag set, so the agent recognises a tool failure and not content.","Incorrect. An empty string replaces a mislabelled failure with a silent one.","Incorrect. An unhandled exception turns a recoverable tool error into a transport-level breakdown."]},
{"id":"Q70","s":6,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"You need to sequence a multi-tool extraction workflow: `extract_metadata` must run first to produce a document classification and ID, then `enrich_data` uses that classification and ID to apply the correct extraction schema. If the model calls `enrich_data` first, it lacks the classification and produces incorrect extractions. How do you guarantee the correct tool sequence?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Add \"always call `extract_metadata` before `enrich_data`\" to the system prompt and reinforce it in both tool descriptions"},{"l":"B","t":"Force `extract_metadata` with `tool_choice` {type: \"tool\", name: \"`extract_metadata`\"}, then allow `enrich_data` in the next turn"},{"l":"C","t":"Merge both tools into a single `extract_and_enrich` tool that runs the two steps in the right order internally"},{"l":"D","t":"Call both tools in parallel and let `extract_metadata` finish first, since it is the faster of the two operations in practice"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Forced tool selection with `tool_choice` guarantees the prerequisite tool executes first. The follow-up turn processes the dependent step after the prerequisite data is available. This provides a deterministic sequencing guarantee that prompt instructions cannot.","w":"A is probabilistic and may be ignored; C loses the modularity benefit of separate tools; D has no ordering guarantee.","x":["Incorrect. A system prompt instruction and reinforced descriptions are still probabilistic and may be ignored.","Correct. Force extract_metadata with tool_choice, then allow enrich_data in the next turn. That is a deterministic guarantee of the order.","Incorrect. Merging them into one tool loses the modularity of separate tools.","Incorrect. Calling both in parallel gives no ordering guarantee."]},
{"id":"Q73","s":4,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Your team has a `fetch_url` tool that accepts any URL and returns the page content. Agents frequently misuse it to fetch non-document URLs (API endpoints, binary files, internal admin pages), causing errors and security concerns. The tool is primarily needed for loading research documents. What is the recommended redesign?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Expand the description to warn agents clearly against fetching API endpoints, binary files and internal admin pages"},{"l":"B","t":"Replace `fetch_url` with a `load_document` tool that only fetches URLs matching an approved document pattern"},{"l":"C","t":"Remove the tool and have engineers paste document contents into the conversation when research needs them"},{"l":"D","t":"Add a routing classifier that reviews and pre-approves each URL before the existing `fetch_url` tool executes it"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Replacing a generic tool with a constrained alternative narrows the tool's attack surface by design. `load_document` can validate that URLs match expected document patterns before fetching, making misuse structurally impossible rather than relying on the agent's judgment.","w":"A is probabilistic guidance; C removes useful capability; D adds complexity for something the tool itself should enforce.","x":["Incorrect. Warning agents in the description is probabilistic guidance.","Correct. Replace fetch_url with a load_document tool that only fetches URLs matching an approved document pattern, which makes misuse structurally impossible.","Incorrect. Removing the tool takes away a useful capability.","Incorrect. A classifier that approves each URL adds complexity for something the tool itself should enforce."]},
{"id":"Q74","s":4,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Your developer productivity agent needs to trace how a function is used across a codebase that heavily uses wrapper modules. The function `calculate_tax` is re-exported through three wrapper files before being called in the actual business logic. Simply grepping for '`calculate_tax`' misses the intermediate wrapper names (`taxHelper`, `computeTax`, `getTaxAmount`). What is the correct multi-step exploration approach?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Read every file in the project in turn and follow the calls by hand, so no wrapper or alias can be missed along the way"},{"l":"B","t":"Find every name the defining file exports `calculate_tax` under, then Grep for each name to follow the wrapper chain"},{"l":"C","t":"Ask the developer which wrapper names the codebase uses, then Grep for each of the names they list"},{"l":"D","t":"Use Glob to find every file with 'tax' in its filename, since wrappers for a tax function are usually named after it"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Tracing through wrapper modules requires following the export chain: identify what names the original file exports, then search for each of those names to find where they're re-exported or consumed. This builds the complete usage graph across indirection layers.","w":"A exhausts context; C outsources the agent's job; D finds files by name but not by usage patterns.","x":["Incorrect. Reading every file by hand exhausts the context.","Correct. Find every name the defining file exports calculate_tax under, then Grep for each name to follow the wrapper chain.","Incorrect. Asking the developer for the wrapper names outsources the agent's job.","Incorrect. A filename search finds files by name and not by how they are used."]},
{"id":"Q83","s":3,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Your synthesis subagent frequently needs to verify specific claims while combining research findings - checking dates, confirming statistics, validating names. Currently, each verification requires a round trip through the coordinator to the web search subagent, adding 2-3 extra turns per task. 85% of verifications are simple fact-checks; 15% require deeper investigation. What is the most effective optimization?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Give the synthesis agent the full set of web search tools, so it can handle every verification directly without round trips"},{"l":"B","t":"Have the synthesis agent collect its verification needs and return them all to the coordinator in one batch at the end of the task"},{"l":"C","t":"Give the synthesis agent a scoped `verify_fact` tool for simple lookups, and keep routing complex checks through the coordinator"},{"l":"D","t":"Have the web search agent proactively cache extra context around each source during initial research"}],"a":["C"],"r":"A scoped `verify_fact` tool handles the 85% simple case directly, eliminating round trips. Complex verifications (15%) still route through the coordinator, preserving the separation of concerns. This applies the principle of least privilege - the synthesis agent gets only what it needs for common cases.","w":"A over-provisions the synthesis agent, violating separation of concerns; B creates blocking dependencies; D relies on speculative caching that can't predict verification needs.","x":["Incorrect. Giving the synthesis agent the full web search toolset over-provisions it and blurs the separation of concerns.","Incorrect. Collecting verifications to the end creates blocking dependencies.","Correct. Give the synthesis agent a scoped verify_fact tool for the simple 85% and keep complex checks routed through the coordinator. This is least privilege.","Incorrect. Speculative caching cannot predict what will need verifying."]},
{"id":"Q87","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Your MCP tool returns error messages as ordinary successful text responses - when a document parsing fails, the tool returns 'Error: could not parse document' as its result text without setting any error flag. The agent then treats that error message as the document's actual content and attempts to 'extract' structured data from the sentence, producing `vendor_name`: 'Error' and amount: 'could not'. What is the correct MCP error pattern?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Prefix every error message with 'SYSTEM ERROR:' so the agent can recognize failures through a consistent text convention"},{"l":"B","t":"Return the failure with the MCP `isError` flag set, so the agent treats it as a tool failure rather than document text"},{"l":"C","t":"Return an empty string on failure, so there is no text for the agent to misinterpret as document content"},{"l":"D","t":"Throw an unhandled exception so the MCP connection drops and the client reconnects and retries from a clean state"}],"a":["B"],"r":"The MCP `isError` flag is the designed channel for communicating tool failures. When set, the agent treats the response as an error condition and can reason about recovery. Without it, error text is structurally indistinguishable from legitimate document content.","w":"A relies on text conventions the model may not interpret correctly; C is a silent failure that hides the problem; D causes a transport-level breakdown when a graceful error signal exists.","x":["Incorrect. A 'SYSTEM ERROR:' prefix depends on text conventions the model may not interpret correctly.","Correct. Return the failure with the MCP isError flag set, so the agent treats it as a tool failure and not as document text.","Incorrect. An empty string is a silent failure that hides the problem.","Incorrect. An unhandled exception causes a transport-level breakdown when a graceful error signal exists."]},
{"id":"Q15","s":2,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"You have a personal `/scratch-notes` slash command you use constantly during development to jot down architectural observations. When a new teammate clones the repository, they see your `/scratch-notes` command in their Claude Code session and are confused by its purpose. Where should personal workflow commands be stored so they don't appear for teammates?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"In `.claude/commands/` in the repository, with a comment at the top of the file marking it as personal"},{"l":"B","t":"In the root `CLAUDE.md` under a # Personal Commands heading, so teammates can see it is yours"},{"l":"C","t":"In ~/`.claude/commands/` in your home directory, which is user-scoped and outside version control"},{"l":"D","t":"In `.claude/rules/` with a paths glob that matches only the files you usually edit yourself"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Command scoping mirrors intent: ~/`.claude/commands/` holds personal commands visible only to you. Commands in `.claude/commands/` are shared with everyone via version control. A personal workflow tool that confuses teammates belongs in the user-scoped directory.","w":"A publishes to the team via VCS; B is for project context, not command definitions; D misuses path-scoped rules for user scoping.","x":["Incorrect. A comment in a file in the repository's commands folder does not stop it being shared with every teammate.","Incorrect. CLAUDE.md holds project context, not command definitions, and it would also be shared.","Correct. The commands folder in your home directory is user-scoped and outside version control, so only you see the command.","Incorrect. Path-scoped rules are for file-specific conventions and are not a way to scope a command to one user."]},
{"id":"Q16","s":2,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Your `/analyze-architecture` skill runs a deep exploration of the codebase, producing thousands of lines of discovery output - file listings, dependency traces, and module analyses. This output floods the main conversation context, pushing out the task you actually wanted help with afterward. Which `SKILL.md` frontmatter option isolates this verbose output?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"argument-hint, so the skill asks which areas to analyze and produces a smaller, targeted report"},{"l":"B","t":"context: fork, which runs the skill in an isolated sub-agent and returns only the distilled results"},{"l":"C","t":"allowed-tools limited to Read, so the skill cannot run the commands that generate most of the output"},{"l":"D","t":"paths set to the architecture files only, which limits how much of the codebase it explores"}],"a":["B"],"r":"context: fork runs the skill in its own isolated context. The main session receives only the distilled result - not the thousands of lines of intermediate exploration. This is the designed mechanism for skills that produce verbose or exploratory output.","w":"A controls parameter prompting not output volume; C limits tools not context pollution; D restricts scope but doesn't isolate output.","x":["Incorrect. argument-hint changes what the skill asks for, not where its output goes.","Correct. context: fork runs the skill in an isolated sub-agent, so the main conversation receives only the distilled result and not thousands of lines of exploration.","Incorrect. Limiting allowed-tools to Read restricts what the skill can do, but its output still lands in the main context.","Incorrect. paths narrows scope but does not isolate the output that remains."]},
{"id":"Q17","s":2,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"You are writing a `/scaffold-component` skill that generates boilerplate files for new React components. Security review requires a guarantee that this skill can create and write files but can never execute shell commands - even if a creative prompt injection attempts to make it run arbitrary bash commands through the skill's instructions. How do you enforce this?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Add a prominent instruction in the `SKILL.md` body: \"NEVER run Bash commands under any circumstances\""},{"l":"B","t":"Trust that the skill's narrow purpose (file generation) will naturally keep it away from shell commands"},{"l":"C","t":"Run the skill only in plan mode, so it can propose files without being able to execute anything"},{"l":"D","t":"Set allowed-tools in the `SKILL.md` frontmatter to Write and Read only, so Bash is unavailable to the skill"}],"a":["D"],"r":"allowed-tools is a deterministic enforcement mechanism: the skill literally cannot invoke tools not in its allowed list, regardless of what its instructions say or what the model attempts. This provides the security guarantee that prompt-level instructions cannot.","w":"A has a non-zero failure rate; B is hope, not security; C changes the workflow mode but doesn't restrict per-skill tool access.","x":["Incorrect. An instruction in the SKILL.md body is prompt-level and has a non-zero failure rate.","Incorrect. Relying on the skill's narrow purpose is hope and not security.","Incorrect. Running in plan mode changes the workflow but does not restrict which tools the skill can use.","Correct. Setting allowed-tools in the SKILL.md frontmatter to Write and Read only makes Bash unavailable to the skill, whatever its instructions say."]},
{"id":"Q18","s":2,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A new engineer joins the team and their Claude Code sessions consistently ignore the team's coding standards - no consistent import ordering, wrong error handling patterns, missing JSDoc comments. You discover the coding standards are documented in your own ~/`.claude/CLAUDE.md` file. What is the correct fix, and how do you verify it works?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Move the standards into the committed project `CLAUDE.md`, then run `/memory` to check which files a session has loaded"},{"l":"B","t":"Send the new engineer your ~/`.claude/CLAUDE.md` to copy into their home directory, then confirm their output matches yours"},{"l":"C","t":"Have the new engineer reinstall Claude Code so it picks up a clean default configuration, then rerun the same task"},{"l":"D","t":"Paste the standards at the start of each session until the model picks them up, then check the next pull request"}],"a":["A"],"r":"User-level `CLAUDE.md` applies only to that user and never travels via version control. Team standards must be in project-level configuration so every clone receives them automatically. The `/memory` command verifies which configuration files a session actually loaded.","w":"B distributes by hand and drifts immediately; C reinstalls software for a file-location problem; D treats a persistent configuration issue as a per-session prompting task.","x":["Correct. A user-level CLAUDE.md applies only to that user. Move the standards into the committed project CLAUDE.md, and use /memory to see which files a session loaded.","Incorrect. Sending your home-directory file to copy distributes by hand, and copies drift immediately.","Incorrect. Reinstalling Claude Code does not fix a file-location problem.","Incorrect. Pasting the standards at the start of every session treats a persistent configuration issue as a per-session task."]},
{"id":"Q19","s":2,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Your project's `CLAUDE.md` has grown to 2,000 lines covering testing conventions, API design patterns, deployment procedures, and code style guidelines. Every Claude Code session loads all 2,000 lines regardless of what the developer is working on - someone fixing a CSS bug loads deployment procedures, and someone writing API handlers loads CSS conventions. What is the recommended reorganization?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Delete the file and rely on the model's general knowledge of best practices, which already covers most of these conventions"},{"l":"B","t":"Move the content into each developer's user-level ~/`.claude/CLAUDE.md` so everyone keeps only the sections relevant to their own work"},{"l":"C","t":"Split it into topic files in `.claude/rules/` (`testing.md`, `api-conventions.md`, `deployment.md`), each with a paths glob in its frontmatter"},{"l":"D","t":"Convert the entire file into a single slash command that developers invoke manually when they need the standards"}],"a":["C"],"r":"The `.claude/rules/` directory is the modular alternative to a monolithic `CLAUDE.md`: focused topic files with path-scoped frontmatter. Testing conventions load only when editing test files, API conventions only for API files - reducing irrelevant context while maintaining coverage.","w":"A abandons hard-won conventions; B unshares team configuration and causes divergence; D makes always-relevant standards opt-in and forgettable.","x":["Incorrect. Deleting the file throws away conventions the team worked out, and the model's general knowledge does not know them.","Incorrect. Moving content to each developer's user-level file removes shared team configuration and makes everyone diverge.","Correct. Split it into topic files in .claude/rules/, each with a paths glob in its frontmatter, so testing conventions load only when editing test files and so on.","Incorrect. A single slash command makes always-relevant standards opt-in, so they will be forgotten."]},
{"id":"Q20","s":2,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Your monorepo contains three packages (frontend, backend, shared-lib), each with different coding conventions. The frontend uses functional React patterns, the backend uses async/await with specific error handling, and shared-lib follows a strict interface-first design. Currently, a single `CLAUDE.md` tries to cover all three with sections like \"## When working in frontend/...\" but Claude frequently applies backend patterns to frontend code. What mechanism lets each package maintain its own standards modularly?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Create a `CLAUDE.md` in each package subdirectory repeating the shared standards plus package-specific ones"},{"l":"B","t":"Use @import in each package's `CLAUDE.md` to pull in only the shared standards it needs, plus its own package rules"},{"l":"C","t":"Create one `.claude/rules/` file per package with no frontmatter, loading all rules for all packages at all times"},{"l":"D","t":"Have developers switch between configuration profiles when they move between packages, one profile per package"}],"a":["B"],"r":"The @import syntax lets each package's `CLAUDE.md` reference only the shared standards files relevant to it, keeping configuration modular and DRY. Each package gets its own combination of shared + package-specific rules without content duplication.","w":"A duplicates shared standards across packages; C loads everything regardless of context; D requires manual intervention that will be forgotten.","x":["Incorrect. A CLAUDE.md in each package that repeats the shared standards duplicates them.","Correct. Use @import in each package's CLAUDE.md to pull in only the shared standards it needs, plus its own package rules.","Incorrect. Rules files with no frontmatter load all rules for all packages at all times.","Incorrect. Switching configuration profiles by hand is manual and will be forgotten."]},
{"id":"Q21","s":2,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Test files in your codebase are spread throughout the directory tree alongside the code they test (e.g., `Button.test.tsx` next to `Button.tsx`, `api.test.ts` next to `api.ts`). You want all test files to follow the same testing conventions - using your fixture library, specific assertion patterns, and coverage requirements - regardless of which directory they're in. What is the most maintainable way to apply these conventions automatically?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Create `.claude/rules/testing.md` with paths: [\"**/*.test.*\", \"**/*.spec.*\"] in its frontmatter"},{"l":"B","t":"Place a separate `CLAUDE.md` file in every directory that contains test files, each with the testing conventions"},{"l":"C","t":"Add the testing conventions to the root `CLAUDE.md` and trust Claude to recognize when it's working on a test file"},{"l":"D","t":"Create a `/apply-test-conventions` slash command that developers must remember to run before writing tests"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Path-specific rules with glob patterns apply conventions based on file naming patterns regardless of directory location. A single rules file with paths: [\"**/*.test.*\"] covers test files scattered throughout the entire codebase automatically.","w":"B requires a `CLAUDE.md` in every directory and doesn't scale; C relies on inference rather than explicit matching; D requires manual invocation that will be forgotten.","x":["Correct. A rules file with paths globs such as **/*.test.* applies the conventions to test files in any directory automatically.","Incorrect. A CLAUDE.md in every directory with tests does not scale and is hard to maintain.","Incorrect. Putting the conventions in the root CLAUDE.md relies on Claude inferring when it is working on a test file instead of matching explicitly.","Incorrect. A slash command that developers must remember to run is manual and will be forgotten."]},
{"id":"Q22","s":2,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"You want to create a `/deploy-checklist` slash command that runs your team's standard pre-deployment verification workflow. This command should be available to every developer automatically when they clone the repository, without any manual setup. Where should the command file be created?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"In ~/`.claude/commands/` in each developer's home directory, with setup instructions in the wiki"},{"l":"B","t":"In `.claude/commands/` in the project repository, so it's version-controlled and available on clone"},{"l":"C","t":"In the root `CLAUDE.md` under a # Commands heading, which every session loads on clone"},{"l":"D","t":"In `.claude/skills/` as a skill with context: fork, which also ships with the repository"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Project-scoped commands in `.claude/commands/` are version-controlled and automatically available to every developer on clone or pull. User-scoped commands (~/`.claude/commands/`) require manual setup per developer.","w":"A requires manual setup per developer; C is for instructions not command definitions; D is a different mechanism - skills vs commands.","x":["Incorrect. A command in each developer's home directory needs manual setup.","Correct. A command in the project's .claude/commands/ is version-controlled and available to every developer on clone or pull.","Incorrect. CLAUDE.md holds instructions and not command definitions.","Incorrect. Skills are a different mechanism from commands."]},
{"id":"Q23","s":2,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Your team wants to enforce that specific instructions are handled differently based on their compliance requirements. Security rules (no hardcoded credentials, no eval()) must be enforced with zero tolerance. Code style preferences (4-space indentation, trailing commas) should guide the model but occasional deviations are acceptable. Currently everything is in `CLAUDE.md`. How should these be restructured?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep everything in `CLAUDE.md` but prefix the security rules with \"CRITICAL:\" so the model treats them as non-negotiable"},{"l":"B","t":"Enforce the security rules with hooks or permission settings, and keep the style preferences in `CLAUDE.md` as guidance"},{"l":"C","t":"Move every rule, security and style alike, into hooks so all of them are enforced the same deterministic way"},{"l":"D","t":"Move style preferences to hooks and keep security rules in `CLAUDE.md` since they're more important to document"}],"a":["B"],"r":"The dividing line is tolerance for violation: security rules requiring zero tolerance belong in hooks or settings permissions (deterministic enforcement). Style preferences that tolerate occasional deviation belong in `CLAUDE.md` (probabilistic guidance). Each mechanism matches its compliance requirement.","w":"A adds emphasis without changing enforcement level; C over-engineers style guidance that doesn't need determinism; D inverts the relationship.","x":["Incorrect. A 'CRITICAL:' prefix adds emphasis but does not change the level of enforcement.","Correct. Enforce the zero-tolerance security rules with hooks or permission settings, and keep style preferences in CLAUDE.md as guidance.","Incorrect. Putting every rule in hooks over-engineers style guidance that can tolerate occasional deviation.","Incorrect. This inverts the relationship, leaving the security rules as mere documentation."]},
{"id":"Q24","s":2,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A developer needs to provide context about a legacy database migration script for a one-time refactoring task. The context includes specific table mappings and column renames that won't be relevant after today. What is the most appropriate method for providing this context to Claude Code?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Add the migration details to the project-level `CLAUDE.md` so it's always available in case the context is needed again"},{"l":"B","t":"Create a `.claude/rules/` file scoped to the migration script's path, so it loads only when that script is edited"},{"l":"C","t":"Give it in the conversation, inline or with an @ reference to the migration notes, since it is only needed for this session"},{"l":"D","t":"Create a custom skill that loads the table mappings and column renames whenever the developer invokes the refactoring task"}],"a":["C"],"r":"For one-time, session-specific context, @ references or inline description in the conversation are appropriate. `CLAUDE.md` and `.claude/rules/` are for reusable, cross-session guidance. Persistent config for a one-time task adds maintenance burden with no future benefit.","w":"A adds permanent config for temporary context; B creates a rules file that will be stale tomorrow; D is over-engineered for a single use.","x":["Incorrect. Adding one-time migration details to the project CLAUDE.md creates permanent configuration for temporary context, and it loads in every session.","Incorrect. A rules file scoped to the migration script would be stale after today and adds maintenance with no future benefit.","Correct. Context needed only for this session belongs in the conversation, either inline or with an @ reference to the migration notes.","Incorrect. A custom skill is over-engineered for a single use."]},
{"id":"Q25","s":2,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Two tasks arrive simultaneously: (1) fixing an off-by-one error in a single pagination function - you have a clear stack trace showing the exact line - and (2) migrating the codebase from Express to Fastify, touching 45+ route handlers with several viable migration strategies. How should plan mode and direct execution be assigned?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Plan mode for both, since planning first improves quality on any change and costs little even on the small bug fix"},{"l":"B","t":"Direct execution for both - plan mode slows down simple fixes and experienced developers don't need it for migrations"},{"l":"C","t":"Execute the one-line bug fix directly, and use plan mode for the 45-handler migration with several viable strategies"},{"l":"D","t":"Plan mode for the bug fix to confirm the root cause first, and direct execution for the migration to keep momentum"}],"a":["C"],"r":"The selection rule: direct execution for simple, well-scoped changes with clear fixes; plan mode when scale, architectural implications, or competing approaches make design-before-commitment valuable. A single-line bug with a stack trace doesn't need a plan; a 45-file migration does.","w":"A adds unnecessary ceremony to a trivial fix; B risks costly rework on a large migration; D inverts the rule for both tasks.","x":["Incorrect. Planning the one-line bug fix adds ceremony to a trivial change that has a clear stack trace.","Incorrect. Direct execution for the 45-handler migration risks costly rework, because there are several viable strategies to compare first.","Correct. Execute the one-line bug fix directly, and use plan mode for the 45-handler migration, where scale and competing approaches make design before commitment valuable.","Incorrect. This inverts the rule. The simple fix needs no plan, and the large migration does."]},
{"id":"Q29","s":2,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A team wants to enforce that after every file edit in Claude Code, the project's formatter (prettier) and linter (eslint) run automatically - even if the model's instructions don't mention formatting. This should happen deterministically for every edit, not just when the model decides to. What mechanism provides this guarantee?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Add \"always run prettier and eslint after editing files\" to `CLAUDE.md` so every session follows it"},{"l":"B","t":"A PostToolUse hook that runs prettier and eslint after every Write or Edit tool call"},{"l":"C","t":"A custom skill that runs prettier and eslint, which developers invoke after making their edits"},{"l":"D","t":"Few-shot examples in `CLAUDE.md` showing the model running the formatter and linter after each edit"}],"a":["B"],"r":"PostToolUse hooks fire automatically after specific tool invocations. By hooking into Write and Edit, formatting and linting run deterministically after every file change - a guarantee that `CLAUDE.md` instructions and few-shot examples cannot provide.","w":"A is probabilistic guidance the model may skip; C requires manual invocation; D is probabilistic and adds context overhead.","x":["Incorrect. An instruction in CLAUDE.md is probabilistic and may be skipped.","Correct. A PostToolUse hook that runs prettier and eslint after every Write or Edit runs deterministically after each change.","Incorrect. A custom skill that developers must invoke is manual.","Incorrect. Few-shot examples are probabilistic and add context overhead."]},
{"id":"Q50","s":4,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"During a multi-phase exploration task, the agent's discovery phase produces thousands of lines of Grep results, file contents, and dependency traces. By the time it reaches the implementation phase, context is exhausted and the agent can barely produce any code. How should the discovery and implementation phases be separated?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise `max_tokens` so the session has enough room to hold the discovery output and still produce the implementation"},{"l":"B","t":"Delegate discovery to an Explore subagent that returns a condensed summary, so implementation starts with a clean context"},{"l":"C","t":"Disable Grep and Glob during the task so discovery cannot produce thousands of lines of output in the first place"},{"l":"D","t":"Keep both phases in one session and run `/compact` between them, so discovery output is compressed before implementation starts"}],"a":["B"],"r":"The Explore subagent isolates verbose discovery output in its own context. The main session receives only the distilled summary - key findings, relevant file paths, architectural observations - preserving its context budget for the implementation phase.","w":"A doesn't solve attention quality; C removes essential exploration tools; D helps but still leaves discovery artifacts in the compressed context.","x":["Incorrect. A larger max_tokens does not solve the quality problem of an overloaded context.","Correct. Delegate discovery to an Explore subagent that returns a condensed summary, so implementation starts with a clean context.","Incorrect. Disabling Grep and Glob removes essential exploration tools.","Incorrect. /compact helps, but discovery artefacts still remain in the compressed context."]},
{"id":"Q52","s":5,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Your CI pipeline script runs claude \"Analyze this pull request for security issues\" but the job hangs indefinitely in the CI environment. Logs show Claude Code is waiting for interactive terminal input. The pipeline eventually times out after 30 minutes. What is the correct way to invoke Claude Code in an automated, non-interactive pipeline?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Set CLAUDE_HEADLESS=true in the job environment before running the command, so Claude Code skips interactive prompts"},{"l":"B","t":"Redirect stdin from `/dev/null` (claude \"Analyze...\" < `/dev/null`) so Claude Code sees no terminal and cannot wait for input"},{"l":"C","t":"Use the `-p` flag: claude `-p` \"Analyze this pull request for security issues\", which prints the result and exits"},{"l":"D","t":"Add the `--batch` flag (claude `--batch` \"Analyze...\") so the prompt is queued and processed without a terminal session"}],"a":["C"],"r":"The `-p` (or `--print`) flag is the documented mechanism for non-interactive execution. It processes the prompt, writes the result to stdout, and exits without waiting for terminal input - exactly what CI/CD pipelines require.","w":"A references a non-existent environment variable; B is a Unix workaround that doesn't properly address Claude Code's interaction model; D references a non-existent flag.","x":["Incorrect. CLAUDE_HEADLESS is not a real environment variable.","Incorrect. Redirecting stdin from /dev/null is a Unix workaround that does not address how Claude Code handles interaction.","Correct. The -p (or --print) flag runs non-interactively, writes the result to stdout and exits, which is what CI needs.","Incorrect. --batch is not a real flag."]},
{"id":"Q53","s":5,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Your CI pipeline generates tests automatically on each PR, but developers reject 80% of them. Rejected tests consistently ignore your team's fixture library (duplicating setup code), test trivial getters/setters, and use assertion patterns your team considers unreadable. The test generation prompt says \"write comprehensive tests for the changed files.\" What is the configuration-level fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Generate three times as many tests per PR so that enough useful ones survive review, and discard the rest automatically"},{"l":"B","t":"Have developers rewrite the generated tests as a standing practice, treating the output as a first draft to be edited"},{"l":"C","t":"Lower the temperature to 0.0 so test generation is deterministic and follows one consistent pattern on every pull request"},{"l":"D","t":"Document the testing standards, the fixture library and example tests in `CLAUDE.md`, which CI-invoked Claude Code loads"}],"a":["D"],"r":"CI invocations get their project context from `CLAUDE.md`. Standards, value criteria, fixture documentation, and example tests directly raise generation quality - the model can only follow conventions it has been shown. Without this context, it falls back to generic test patterns.","w":"A scales the reject pile; B institutionalizes rework instead of fixing the cause; C reduces variability of output that is uninformed either way.","x":["Incorrect. Generating three times as many tests only grows the pile that gets rejected.","Incorrect. Having developers rewrite the output institutionalises rework and does not fix the cause.","Incorrect. Temperature 0.0 makes output consistent, but it stays uninformed about the team's standards.","Correct. Document the testing standards, the fixture library and example tests in CLAUDE.md, which CI-invoked Claude Code loads, so the model has the conventions to follow."]},
{"id":"Q54","s":5,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Your automated review bot re-runs after each new commit to a PR. After the third commit, developers have muted the bot because it re-posts the exact same 12 findings on every run - even findings they've already addressed. The signal-to-noise ratio has collapsed. How should incremental re-reviews be designed?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Pass the prior findings into context and report only new issues or earlier findings that are still unaddressed"},{"l":"B","t":"Review only the latest commit's diff in isolation, so each run reports just what that commit changed and nothing older"},{"l":"C","t":"Limit the bot to one review per PR, run on the first push, so developers only ever see a single set of findings"},{"l":"D","t":"Re-post all findings but with different formatting, such as bullets instead of a numbered list, so each run looks fresh"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Duplicate suppression is a context design problem: provide the reviewer with its own prior findings and explicitly instruct it to report deltas - new issues plus unresolved carryovers. This maintains complete coverage while eliminating the spam that causes developers to mute the bot.","w":"B misses issues that emerge from interaction between commits; C leaves everything after the first push unreviewed; D disguises the spam developers have already rejected.","x":["Correct. Pass the prior findings into context and report only new issues or earlier findings that are still unaddressed.","Incorrect. Reviewing only the latest diff in isolation misses issues that come from interactions between commits.","Incorrect. One review on the first push leaves later commits unreviewed.","Incorrect. Reformatting the same findings disguises the spam developers have already rejected."]},
{"id":"Q76","s":5,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Your CI pipeline generates tests for changed code, but the generated tests frequently suggest test scenarios that are already covered by the existing test suite, wasting reviewer time on duplicates. What should you provide in the CI context to prevent this?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Generate tests only for files that have no existing tests at all, so new tests never overlap with the current suite"},{"l":"B","t":"Include the existing test files in context so generation can see which scenarios are already covered"},{"l":"C","t":"Limit generation to one new test per changed file, so the number of possible duplicates stays small and easy to review"},{"l":"D","t":"Have developers delete generated tests that duplicate existing ones as a standing practice"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Providing existing test files in context lets the generator see what's already covered and focus on genuinely missing scenarios. Without this visibility, it proposes tests from scratch and inevitably duplicates existing coverage.","w":"A skips files that might need additional test scenarios; C artificially limits coverage; D institutionalizes rework instead of preventing it.","x":["Incorrect. Generating tests only for files with no tests skips files that may still need extra scenarios.","Correct. Include the existing test files in context so the generator can see which scenarios are already covered.","Incorrect. Limiting generation to one test per file artificially cuts coverage.","Incorrect. Having developers delete duplicates institutionalises rework instead of preventing it."]},
{"id":"Q79","s":2,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A developer wants to use Claude Code's interview pattern before implementing a caching layer in an unfamiliar domain. They want Claude to ask probing questions about cache invalidation strategies, failure modes, and consistency requirements before writing any code. How should this interaction be structured?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Write a detailed specification up front covering all the caching concerns you know of, then have Claude implement it"},{"l":"B","t":"Ask Claude to implement caching right away and iterate on the bugs that testing uncovers, since real failures teach fastest"},{"l":"C","t":"Ask Claude to interview you first, raising questions about invalidation, failure modes and consistency before it writes code"},{"l":"D","t":"Pick an established caching library and have Claude integrate it directly, since the library already encodes the hard design choices"}],"a":["C"],"r":"The interview pattern has Claude ask probing questions to surface design considerations before implementation. In unfamiliar domains like caching, this identifies invalidation strategies, failure modes, and consistency requirements the developer may not have considered - leading to a more robust implementation.","w":"A assumes the developer already knows all the concerns; B discovers problems through bugs rather than design; D skips the design discussion entirely.","x":["Incorrect. A specification up front assumes the developer already knows all the concerns, which is not true in an unfamiliar domain.","Incorrect. Implementing straight away finds problems through bugs and not through design.","Correct. Ask Claude to interview you first about invalidation, failure modes and consistency before it writes code.","Incorrect. Integrating a library skips the design discussion the developer wanted."]},
{"id":"Q80","s":2,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Your test suite has failing edge cases: a migration script crashes on null values in the source data. You want Claude Code to fix the handling. Should you describe the problem in prose, or provide something more concrete?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Describe the problem clearly in prose, for example that the migration script does not handle null values properly"},{"l":"B","t":"Provide failing test cases with sample input containing nulls and the expected output, so Claude can target the fix"},{"l":"C","t":"Lower the temperature so the generated fix is more precise and less likely to introduce unrelated changes to the script"},{"l":"D","t":"Ask Claude to rewrite the migration script from scratch with null handling built in, rather than patching the old code"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Specific test cases with example input and expected output communicate the exact behavior boundary far more reliably than prose descriptions. Claude can see the null value that causes the crash, the expected handling, and write a targeted fix.","w":"A is vague about which null values and what handling is expected; C doesn't improve understanding of the problem; D is overkill for an edge case fix.","x":["Incorrect. A prose description is vague about which null values occur and what handling is expected.","Correct. Provide failing test cases with sample input containing nulls and the expected output, so Claude can target the fix.","Incorrect. Temperature does not improve Claude's understanding of the problem.","Incorrect. Rewriting the whole script is overkill for an edge case fix."]},
{"id":"Q93","s":4,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"You are about to restructure a monolithic application into microservices, touching dozens of files with decisions about service boundaries, API contracts, and data ownership. A colleague suggests using direct execution to 'move fast.' Why is this the wrong approach, and what should you use instead?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Direct execution is fine for any task, provided the instructions given up front are detailed enough to remove ambiguity"},{"l":"B","t":"Use plan mode, so you can explore the codebase and agree service boundaries and contracts before costly changes begin"},{"l":"C","t":"Neither: do the design in a separate architecture tool outside Claude Code, then bring the finished plan back in to execute"},{"l":"D","t":"Direct execution with frequent git commits, so any wrong turn can be reverted quickly without losing much work"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Plan mode is designed for exactly this class of task: large-scale changes with multiple valid approaches, architectural implications, and high cost of reversal. It enables exploration and design before commitment, preventing costly rework that 'move fast' direct execution would cause.","w":"A underestimates architectural complexity; C unnecessarily moves outside the tool; D uses git as a safety net instead of designing before committing.","x":["Incorrect. Detailed instructions do not remove the architectural uncertainty of a dozens-of-files restructure.","Correct. Use plan mode to explore the codebase and agree service boundaries and contracts before costly changes begin.","Incorrect. Moving the design to another tool takes it outside Claude Code unnecessarily.","Incorrect. Frequent commits are a safety net for wrong turns and not a replacement for designing first."]},
{"id":"Q98","s":2,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Your team wants multiple interacting bug fixes addressed in one Claude Code session. Three bugs interact: a race condition in the event queue causes a null pointer in the handler, which triggers an incorrect error message to the user. A fourth bug (a typo in a config file) is completely independent. How should these be addressed?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Fix all four bugs one at a time in four separate messages, so each change can be reviewed and tested in isolation"},{"l":"B","t":"Describe all four bugs in one message with equal detail, so Claude has the complete picture before changing anything"},{"l":"C","t":"Handle the three interacting bugs together in one detailed message, and fix the independent config typo separately"},{"l":"D","t":"Let Claude decide the order based on its own assessment of priority, since it can see the code and judge the dependencies"}],"a":["C"],"r":"When fixes interact (the race condition, null pointer, and error message are causally linked), addressing them together gives Claude the full picture of how changes propagate. Independent issues (the config typo) don't benefit from this shared context and can be handled separately.","w":"A loses the interaction context between related bugs; B mixes unrelated issues with interacting ones; D delegates sequencing without providing the interaction information.","x":["Incorrect. Fixing the interacting bugs separately loses the context of how they affect each other.","Incorrect. Describing all four with equal detail mixes an unrelated issue with the interacting ones.","Correct. Handle the three interacting bugs together in one detailed message, and fix the independent config typo separately.","Incorrect. Leaving Claude to decide the order gives it no information about how the bugs interact."]},
{"id":"Q55","s":5,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"Your review prompt says \"be conservative and only report high-confidence findings\" yet false positives remain at 35%, particularly in the data validation category. Adding \"be very, very conservative\" to the prompt had no measurable effect. What does effective precision engineering look like for automated code review?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Add \"CRITICAL: minimize false positives\" in capitals at the top of the prompt so the instruction takes priority"},{"l":"B","t":"Report all findings but sort them by the model's self-reported confidence score, letting developers focus on the top of the list"},{"l":"C","t":"Define explicit categories to report (bugs, security, data loss) and to skip (style, naming), with concrete boundary examples"},{"l":"D","t":"Review fewer files per run so the model can give each file more attention and make fewer careless false-positive calls"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Vague conservatism doesn't transfer to the model - it has no operational definition of \"conservative.\" Specific categorical criteria with concrete boundaries give the model a clear, actionable decision rule: report bugs and security issues, skip style nitpicks. This is what actually moves the precision needle.","w":"A intensifies an instruction that already has no operational content; B reorders noise rather than reducing it; D reviews less code with the same faulty judgment.","x":["Incorrect. Capital letters intensify an instruction that has no operational meaning.","Incorrect. Sorting by self-reported confidence reorders the noise without reducing it.","Correct. Define explicit categories to report (bugs, security, data loss) and to skip (style, naming), with concrete boundary examples.","Incorrect. Reviewing fewer files per run applies the same faulty judgement to less code."]},
{"id":"Q56","s":5,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"Your reviewer consistently flags intentional code patterns as bugs - for example, intentional switch fall-throughs marked with /* falls through */ comments, and empty catch blocks with // intentionally swallowed comments. Detailed prose instructions like \"do not flag intentional patterns\" haven't fixed the problem because the model can't reliably distinguish \"intentional\" from \"accidental\" based on prose alone. What is the most effective addition?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A rule that any pattern carrying an explanatory code comment is acceptable and should be left out of the review findings"},{"l":"B","t":"An instruction to use good engineering judgment about which patterns are intentional, backed by a note on the team's style"},{"l":"C","t":"A few paired examples, such as a commented intentional fall-through (skip) next to an accidental one (report), each with reasoning"},{"l":"D","t":"Exclude files containing switch statements or empty catch blocks from review, since that is where nearly all of the false positives cluster"}],"a":["C"],"r":"When prose fails to convey a judgment boundary, contrasted few-shot examples carry it. Showing matched pairs of acceptable vs. problematic patterns with explicit reasoning teaches a distinction the model can generalize to novel code - the documented strength of few-shot prompting for false positive reduction.","w":"A creates a rule any commented bug defeats; B restates the goal without providing the judgment; D removes exactly the code that most needs careful review.","x":["Incorrect. A rule that any commented pattern is acceptable can be defeated by a commented bug.","Incorrect. Telling it to use good judgement restates the goal without supplying the distinction.","Correct. Paired examples, such as a commented intentional fall-through to skip next to an accidental one to report, with reasoning, teach a boundary that prose could not.","Incorrect. Excluding files with switch statements or empty catch blocks removes the code that most needs careful review."]},
{"id":"Q57","s":5,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"Your team wants to reduce API costs for two automated workflows: (1) a nightly technical debt report generated overnight for review the next morning, and (2) a blocking pre-merge check that must complete before developers can merge their PRs. Your manager proposes switching both to the Message Batches API for its 50% cost savings. How should you evaluate this proposal?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Switch both workflows to batch processing, since a 50% saving outweighs the extra latency for each of them"},{"l":"B","t":"Batch only the overnight report; pre-merge checks stay synchronous, since a batch can take up to 24 hours with no latency SLA"},{"l":"C","t":"Keep both on the synchronous API, since batch processing cannot correlate the requests it receives with their responses"},{"l":"D","t":"Switch both to batch, with an automatic fallback to the synchronous API whenever a batch takes longer than two hours to return"}],"a":["B"],"r":"The batch API's up to 24-hour processing window with no latency SLA makes it unsuitable for blocking developer workflows where someone is waiting. Overnight reports are the ideal batch profile - latency-tolerant, non-blocking, and cost-sensitive. Each workflow matches to the API that fits its latency requirements.","w":"A blocks developers behind a 24-hour window; C is incorrect - `custom_id` fields exist for request/response correlation; D adds complexity for a problem that has a clean architectural split.","x":["Incorrect. Batching both would put the blocking pre-merge check behind a processing window of up to 24 hours.","Correct. Batch only the overnight report. Pre-merge checks stay synchronous, because a batch can take up to 24 hours with no latency guarantee.","Incorrect. Batch processing can correlate requests and responses, using custom_id.","Incorrect. Batching both with a fallback adds complexity to a problem that has a clean split."]},
{"id":"Q58","s":5,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"You notice that when the same Claude Code session generates code and then reviews it, the review is conspicuously gentle - approving patterns that an independent reviewer flags as problematic. The generated code has an off-by-one error in a loop boundary that the same-session review calls \"a reasonable implementation choice\" while a fresh instance immediately identifies it as a bug. What explains this, and what is the design implication?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The model is being polite to its own work, so the review prompt should tell it to review harshly and assume bugs exist"},{"l":"B","t":"The generating session keeps the reasoning that produced the code, so it rarely questions it; run reviews in an independent instance"},{"l":"C","t":"Reviews should run on a larger, more capable model tier than the generator so they catch what the generator missed"},{"l":"D","t":"Leave at least 24 hours between generation and review so the session's earlier context decays and the reviewer sees the code with genuinely fresh eyes"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Self-review bias stems from shared reasoning context: the model that produced the code retains the logic that justified each decision, making it inherently less likely to identify problems. An independent instance evaluates the code on its own terms, without the generator's confirming context.","w":"A misreads a structural context effect as a personality setting; C changes capability when the problem is contaminated context; D incorrectly assumes context degrades over time.","x":["Incorrect. This is a structural context effect, not politeness, so a harsher prompt is not the right fix.","Correct. The generating session keeps the reasoning that produced the code and rarely questions it, so run reviews in an independent instance.","Incorrect. A larger tier changes capability, but the problem is the contaminated context.","Incorrect. Context does not decay with time, so waiting 24 hours changes nothing."]},
{"id":"Q59","s":5,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"After processing a batch of 10,000 documents overnight, 200 fail: most are oversized documents that exceeded context limits, plus some transient API timeouts. You need to handle these failures efficiently without reprocessing the 9,800 documents that succeeded. How do you identify and resubmit only the failures?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Resubmit the full 10,000-document batch and drop the duplicate results for the 9,800 documents that already succeeded"},{"l":"B","t":"Find the 200 failures by `custom_id`, fix the causes (for example, chunk oversized documents), and resubmit only those requests"},{"l":"C","t":"Accept the 200 failures as a 2% loss rate and proceed with the 9,800 successful results, noting the gap in the run report"},{"l":"D","t":"Send the 200 failures through the synchronous Messages API instead, which handles oversized documents and timeouts more gracefully"}],"a":["B"],"r":"`custom_id` fields exist precisely for this: correlating each request with its response, identifying failures, fixing their causes (chunking oversized docs, retrying after transient errors), and resubmitting only the corrected subset. This is efficient and addresses root causes.","w":"A reprocesses 9,800 successes to handle 200 failures; C silently drops 2% of the corpus; D misunderstands that context limits apply to the model regardless of API mode.","x":["Incorrect. Resubmitting all 10,000 reprocesses the 9,800 that succeeded.","Correct. Find the 200 failures by custom_id, fix the causes (for example, chunk oversized documents), and resubmit only those.","Incorrect. Accepting a 2% loss silently drops part of the corpus.","Incorrect. Context limits apply to the model whichever API is used, so the synchronous API does not fix oversized documents."]},
{"id":"Q60","s":6,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"Your extraction pipeline currently asks Claude to \"respond with JSON\" in the system prompt. Downstream systems parsing the output break regularly because responses sometimes include markdown code fences (```json ... ```), trailing conversational text (\"Here's the extracted data: ...\"), and occasionally malformed JSON syntax. What is the most reliable structural fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Strengthen the prompt: \"Respond ONLY with valid JSON, no markdown, no commentary\", and repeat it at the end"},{"l":"B","t":"Post-process the output with regex to strip code fences and trailing text, then run a JSON repair step on what remains"},{"l":"C","t":"Define the extraction as a tool whose input schema is your JSON schema, and read the data from the `tool_use` block"},{"l":"D","t":"Switch the output format from JSON to XML, which models tend to produce more carefully and which parses more forgivingly"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Tool use with JSON schemas is the reliability mechanism for structured output: the model's extraction arrives as schema-validated tool input, not as free text that happens to contain JSON. Syntax errors and extraneous text are eliminated by construction, not by post-processing.","w":"A improves odds within a fundamentally text-shaped channel; B patches symptoms with fragile regex; D relocates the same free-text problem to a different syntax.","x":["Incorrect. A stronger instruction improves the odds but the output is still free text.","Incorrect. Regex clean-up and JSON repair patch the symptoms and are fragile.","Correct. Define the extraction as a tool whose input schema is your JSON schema and read the data from the tool_use block. The structure comes from the schema, not from post-processing.","Incorrect. Switching to XML relocates the same free-text problem to a different syntax."]},
{"id":"Q61","s":6,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"You have separate extraction tools for invoices, contracts, and medical records - each with its own JSON schema. Incoming documents arrive with unknown types, and the system must always produce a structured extraction (never a conversational text response). The model should determine which schema fits each document. Which `tool_choice` setting is correct?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"`tool_choice`: \"auto\", letting the model decide whether a tool call or a text reply suits each document"},{"l":"B","t":"`tool_choice`: \"any\", so the model must call a tool but can pick which extraction schema fits"},{"l":"C","t":"`tool_choice` forced to the invoice extraction tool for all documents, since invoices are the most common type"},{"l":"D","t":"Omit `tool_choice` entirely and rely on the system prompt instruction to \"always extract structured data\""}],"a":["B"],"r":"\"any\" compels a tool call while preserving the model's choice among schemas - exactly right for unknown document types that must always yield structured output. \"auto\" (and omitting `tool_choice`, which defaults to auto) allows conversational text responses, breaking the pipeline.","w":"A and D permit text responses that break downstream systems; C applies a single schema to all document types regardless of fit.","x":["Incorrect. 'auto' lets the model reply in text, which breaks the pipeline.","Correct. 'any' forces a tool call while the model still picks which extraction schema fits the document.","Incorrect. Forcing the invoice tool applies one schema to every document type regardless of fit.","Incorrect. Omitting tool_choice defaults to auto, and an instruction in the prompt does not enforce a tool call."]},
{"id":"Q62","s":6,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"Your invoice extraction schema marks `vendor_tax_id` as a required field. On approximately 15% of invoices (particularly from small vendors and international suppliers), no tax ID appears anywhere in the document. The model fabricates plausible-looking tax IDs to satisfy the required field constraint, and these fabrications pass format validation but fail when checked against government databases. What is the schema-level fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Tell the model in the prompt to enter \"NOT FOUND\" as the tax ID whenever none is visible in the document"},{"l":"B","t":"Post-validate all tax IDs against a government database and discard failures - catching fabrications after the fact"},{"l":"C","t":"Lower the temperature so any fabricated tax IDs come out less creative and are easier for reviewers to spot"},{"l":"D","t":"Make `vendor_tax_id` optional or nullable, so the model can return null when no tax ID appears in the document"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Required fields on possibly-absent information structurally force fabrication - the schema offers no honest answer. Making the field nullable gives the model a legitimate way to represent \"this information doesn't exist in the source,\" which is the truthful extraction.","w":"A provides a workaround but doesn't fix the structural pressure; B catches fabrications after they've entered the pipeline; C makes fabrications less creative, not less fabricated.","x":["Incorrect. Entering 'NOT FOUND' in the field is a workaround, and a required field still pushes the model to fill it.","Incorrect. Checking tax IDs against a government database after the fact catches fabrications only after they have entered the pipeline.","Incorrect. A lower temperature makes fabrications less creative, not less fabricated.","Correct. A required field on possibly absent information forces fabrication. Make vendor_tax_id optional or nullable so the model can honestly return null."]},
{"id":"Q63","s":6,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"Since adopting `tool_use` with JSON schemas, your extractions are always syntactically valid - no more parsing errors. However, quality review reveals that on 8% of invoices, line item amounts don't sum to the stated total, and vendor names occasionally appear in the address field. The engineering team expected `tool_use` to prevent all extraction errors. What should you understand and do?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Tool use with schemas is meant to prevent semantic errors too, so the extraction tool definition must be misconfigured somewhere"},{"l":"B","t":"Schemas guarantee structure, not semantic correctness; add semantic checks, such as comparing calculated and stated totals"},{"l":"C","t":"Make every field an unconstrained string, so the model never has to force a value into the wrong type or structure"},{"l":"D","t":"Re-run each extraction until the line items sum to the stated total, then keep the first run that passes that check"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Tool use draws a clear boundary: it guarantees structure (valid JSON, correct types, required fields present) but not truth (values being logically correct, fields containing appropriate data). Semantic validation - cross-checks, calculated totals, consistency flags - is a separate necessary layer.","w":"A misunderstands the guarantee's scope; C destroys the structural benefits; D retries until errors align by chance.","x":["Incorrect. Schemas are not meant to prevent semantic errors, so the tool is not misconfigured.","Correct. Schemas guarantee structure, not semantic correctness. Add semantic checks such as comparing calculated and stated totals.","Incorrect. Making every field an unconstrained string throws away the structural benefits.","Incorrect. Re-running until the totals happen to match retries until errors line up by chance."]},
{"id":"Q64","s":6,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"An extraction fails Pydantic validation with two specific field errors: the date field contains \"next Tuesday\" instead of ISO format, and the amount field has \"approximately $500\" instead of a numeric value. You plan to retry. What should the retry request contain, and when should you skip retrying altogether?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Just the instruction to try again more carefully, since the model still remembers the document from the previous attempt"},{"l":"B","t":"Only the two validation error messages without the document, keeping the retry prompt short so the model focuses on the two failed fields"},{"l":"C","t":"Send the source document, the failed output and the specific errors; skip retrying when the data is absent from the document"},{"l":"D","t":"A different source document, so the model gets a fresh start on an easier example before returning to the failed one later"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Retry-with-error-feedback works because the model sees what it produced, exactly what was wrong, and the source document to correct against. The critical boundary: format/structural errors (\"next Tuesday\" → ISO date) are retryable; genuinely absent information is not - no retry can extract what isn't there.","w":"A retries blind without showing the errors; B removes the source document the correction requires; D abandons the failed document instead of fixing the extraction.","x":["Incorrect. Telling the model to try again without showing the errors is a blind retry, and the model does not remember the document.","Incorrect. Sending only the error messages without the source document removes what the correction needs.","Correct. Send the source document, the failed output and the specific errors. Skip retrying when the data is absent from the document, because no retry can extract what is not there.","Incorrect. A different document abandons the failed extraction instead of fixing it."]},
{"id":"Q65","s":6,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"Your extraction schema needs a `document_category` field. Most documents fall into well-defined categories (invoice, contract, receipt, statement), but approximately 5% don't fit any predefined category - cover letters, internal memos, handwritten notes. Currently the model forces these into the closest predefined category, creating systematic misclassification. What schema design pattern accommodates novel categories?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Make `document_category` a free-text string, so the model can describe any document accurately in its own words"},{"l":"B","t":"An enum of the predefined categories plus \"other\", paired with a `category_detail` string describing the novel type"},{"l":"C","t":"Add every document type you can think of to the enum, including rare ones, so the model never lacks a matching value"},{"l":"D","t":"Remove the category field, since a fixed list will misclassify some documents anyway and downstream code can infer the type"}],"a":["B"],"r":"The enum + \"other\" + detail string pattern provides structured categorization for known values while gracefully handling novel categories. The enum constrains the common cases; \"other\" + detail captures edge cases with descriptive text rather than forced misclassification.","w":"A loses all categorization structure; C can't anticipate every possible document type; D removes a useful field instead of designing it properly.","x":["Incorrect. A free-text category loses all categorisation structure.","Correct. An enum of the predefined categories plus 'other', with a category_detail string describing the novel type, handles known and novel documents.","Incorrect. No enum can anticipate every possible document type.","Incorrect. Removing the field discards useful information instead of designing it properly."]},
{"id":"Q66","s":6,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"An extraction pipeline processes documents with varied date formats (\"03/15/2025\", \"15-Mar-25\", \"March 15th, 2025\", \"Q1 2025\"). Extracted dates are inconsistent - sometimes MM/DD/YYYY, sometimes DD/MM/YYYY, sometimes natural language. How do you ensure consistent output formatting across all input variations?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Make the date fields optional so the model can leave out any date it cannot parse with confidence, rather than guess"},{"l":"B","t":"Instruct the model to output dates as YYYY-MM-DD and add few-shot examples parsing each of the varied input formats"},{"l":"C","t":"Post-process every extracted date with a regex-based parser that converts whatever format the model returns into ISO 8601"},{"l":"D","t":"Accept only documents that already use ISO-formatted dates and route everything else to a manual review queue"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Format normalization instructions specify the expected output format, and few-shot examples demonstrate correct handling of each input variant. Together they teach the model to produce consistent YYYY-MM-DD output regardless of how dates appear in the source.","w":"A avoids the problem instead of solving it; C is fragile with varied input formats; D rejects valid documents.","x":["Incorrect. Making date fields optional avoids the problem instead of solving it.","Correct. Instruct the model to output YYYY-MM-DD and add few-shot examples that parse each varied input format.","Incorrect. A regex parser over whatever the model returns is fragile with varied formats.","Incorrect. Accepting only ISO-dated documents rejects valid documents."]},
{"id":"Q75","s":5,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"Your automated review system needs to produce findings that can be automatically posted as inline PR comments by your CI pipeline. Currently, reviews output prose paragraphs that require manual parsing. What CI invocation produces machine-parseable structured findings?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"claude 'review this PR' piped through grep, extracting file paths, line numbers and severities from the prose findings"},{"l":"B","t":"claude `-p` 'review this PR' `--output-format` json `--json-schema`, giving findings the pipeline can parse and post directly"},{"l":"C","t":"claude `--batch` 'review this PR' --format=structured, which queues the review and emits structured findings"},{"l":"D","t":"claude `-p` 'review this PR' on its own, then regex-parse the printed text for file paths, line numbers and findings"}],"a":["B"],"r":"The CI trio: `-p` for non-interactive execution, `--output-format` json for machine-readable output, and `--json-schema` to enforce the exact findings structure your pipeline consumes. No prose parsing, no input hangs, no fragile regex.","w":"A hangs waiting for interactive input; C references non-existent flags; D solves the hang but leaves the pipeline regex-parsing unstructured text.","x":["Incorrect. Without -p the command hangs waiting for input, and grep over prose is fragile.","Correct. Use -p for non-interactive execution, --output-format json for machine-readable output and --json-schema to enforce the findings structure.","Incorrect. --batch and --format=structured are not real flags.","Incorrect. -p alone avoids the hang but leaves the pipeline parsing unstructured text with regex."]},
{"id":"Q77","s":5,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"Your review system's severity classification is inconsistent - nearly identical issues get labeled 'critical' one day and 'minor' the next, making severity-based merge gates unreliable. Developers can't trust the severity labels. How do you achieve consistent classification?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Remove severity levels and treat every finding equally, so merge gates no longer depend on inconsistent labels"},{"l":"B","t":"Let the model assign severity freely, but run five independent reviews and use the average label for each finding as the final one"},{"l":"C","t":"Define each severity level with explicit criteria and concrete examples, such as auth bypass for critical and naming for minor"},{"l":"D","t":"Map severity to the number of lines the issue affects, so the label comes from an objective measure of each change"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Consistency comes from operational definitions: severity levels anchored by concrete code examples give the classifier something to match rather than interpret. This turns subjective judgment into a rubric, making severity gates dependable.","w":"A discards the signal merge gates need; B statistically launders an unanchored judgment; D measures code size, not impact - a one-line auth bypass outranks a fifty-line comment tweak.","x":["Incorrect. Treating every finding equally throws away the signal that merge gates need.","Incorrect. Averaging five reviews launders an unanchored judgement without making it consistent.","Correct. Define each severity level with explicit criteria and concrete examples, for example auth bypass as critical and naming as minor.","Incorrect. Lines affected measure size, not impact, since a one-line auth bypass outranks a long comment edit."]},
{"id":"Q78","s":5,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"The 'code style' finding category in your automated reviewer has a 70% false positive rate. Developers have begun dismissing all findings - including accurate security findings - because trust in the entire system has collapsed. What is the right operational response?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ship more style findings so developers see how much the category catches, which rebuilds confidence in it over time"},{"l":"B","t":"Turn off the style category for now to restore trust in the accurate categories, and tune it offline before re-enabling"},{"l":"C","t":"Rename 'code style' to 'code quality' so developers read the findings as substantive rather than cosmetic"},{"l":"D","t":"Ask developers to tolerate the noise during the tuning period, and track dismissals so the prompts can be improved later"}],"a":["B"],"r":"High false-positive categories are contagious - they teach developers to dismiss everything. Disabling the offender protects the credibility of accurate categories (like security) while the noisy category's prompts are improved offline. Trust is the system's real asset.","w":"A doubles down on trust-destroying noise; C relabels the same false positives; D asks humans to absorb a cost the system's configuration should eliminate.","x":["Incorrect. Shipping more noisy style findings deepens the loss of trust.","Correct. Turn the style category off to protect trust in the accurate categories, and tune it offline before re-enabling.","Incorrect. Renaming the category relabels the same false positives.","Incorrect. Asking developers to tolerate the noise puts a cost on them that the configuration should remove."]},
{"id":"Q88","s":6,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"Your extraction system processes a batch of 10,000 invoices overnight. The batch completes with 200 failures - investigation shows most are oversized documents that exceeded context limits, plus some transient API timeouts. How do you handle the failures without reprocessing the 9,800 successful extractions?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Resubmit the full 10,000-document batch and keep whichever set of results arrives first for each of the invoices"},{"l":"B","t":"Accept the 200 failures as a 2% loss rate and continue with the 9,800 successes, since the sample is statistically sound"},{"l":"C","t":"Find the 200 failures by `custom_id`, fix their causes (chunk oversized documents, retry timeouts), and resubmit only those"},{"l":"D","t":"Send the 200 failures through the synchronous Messages API instead, which copes better with oversized documents and timeouts"}],"a":["C"],"r":"`custom_id` fields exist precisely for this: correlating each request with its response, identifying failures by their specific cause, fixing each cause appropriately (chunking for oversized, retrying for transient), and resubmitting only the corrected subset.","w":"A reprocesses 9,800 successes; B silently drops 2% of the corpus; D misunderstands that context limits apply to the model regardless of which API mode is used.","x":["Incorrect. Resubmitting all 10,000 reprocesses the successful 9,800.","Incorrect. Accepting a 2% loss silently drops part of the corpus.","Correct. Find the 200 failures by custom_id, fix their causes (chunk oversized documents, retry timeouts) and resubmit only those.","Incorrect. Context limits apply to the model whichever API is used, so the synchronous API does not fix oversized documents."]},
{"id":"Q89","s":6,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"Your extraction tool produces valid JSON via `tool_use`, but quality review reveals that on 8% of invoices, the line item amounts don't sum to the stated total, and vendor names occasionally appear in the address field. The engineering team expected `tool_use` to prevent all errors. What is the correct understanding?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Tool use with schemas is supposed to prevent semantic errors as well, so the tool definition must be misconfigured somewhere"},{"l":"B","t":"Schemas guarantee structure and types, not logical correctness; add semantic checks such as calculated versus stated totals"},{"l":"C","t":"Make every field an unconstrained string, so the model is never forced to fit a value into the wrong type or field"},{"l":"D","t":"Re-run each extraction until the line items sum to the stated total, and keep the first result that passes the check"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Tool use draws a clear boundary: it guarantees structure (valid JSON, correct types, required fields present) but not truth (values being logically correct). Semantic validation - cross-checks, calculated totals, field consistency flags - is a necessary additional layer.","w":"A misunderstands what `tool_use` guarantees; C destroys the structural benefits; D retries until errors coincidentally align.","x":["Incorrect. Schemas are not supposed to prevent semantic errors, so the tool definition is not misconfigured.","Correct. Schemas guarantee structure and types, not logical correctness. Add semantic checks such as calculated versus stated totals.","Incorrect. Making every field an unconstrained string destroys the structural benefits.","Incorrect. Re-running until the totals happen to match retries until errors coincidentally line up."]},
{"id":"Q90","s":6,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"You want to guarantee that every API request produces a structured extraction and never a conversational text response. You have three extraction tools (one per document type). Incoming documents have unknown types, and the model should choose the correct tool. What `tool_choice` setting achieves this?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"`tool_choice`: 'auto', so the model can decide for each document whether a tool call or a text reply fits best"},{"l":"B","t":"`tool_choice`: 'any', so the model must call one of the tools but can choose which extraction schema fits"},{"l":"C","t":"Force one specific extraction tool for every document, and let downstream code handle any type that does not fit"},{"l":"D","t":"Omit `tool_choice` and add 'always use a tool' to the system prompt, which the model follows in nearly every case"}],"a":["B"],"r":"'any' compels a tool call while preserving the model's choice among available tools - exactly right for unknown document types that must always yield structured output. 'auto' and omitting `tool_choice` both allow conversational text responses that break the pipeline.","w":"A and D permit text responses; C forces one schema on all document types regardless of fit.","x":["Incorrect. 'auto' lets the model reply in text instead of calling a tool, which breaks the pipeline.","Correct. 'any' forces the model to call one of the tools while it still chooses which extraction schema fits.","Incorrect. Forcing one specific tool applies one schema to every document type regardless of fit.","Incorrect. Omitting tool_choice and adding an instruction still permits conversational replies."]},
{"id":"Q97","s":5,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"You are adding few-shot examples to your code review prompt. A colleague suggests including 20+ examples to cover every possible case. Another suggests just 2-3 targeted examples. Which approach is more effective, and what should the examples demonstrate?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Include 20 or more examples so every type of finding is covered, since broader coverage gives the model more patterns to learn from"},{"l":"B","t":"Two to four examples aimed at the genuinely ambiguous cases, showing the reasoning behind each call and the exact output format"},{"l":"C","t":"Add examples and remove the explicit review criteria, since well-chosen examples replace written rules entirely"},{"l":"D","t":"Skip examples for code review, since few-shot prompting mainly helps prose tasks and adds little to structured analysis"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Effective few-shot prompting is targeted, not exhaustive: a handful of examples aimed at genuine ambiguity with explicit reasoning, plus format demonstrations that lock output structure. 20+ examples bloat context and dilute the signal of the examples that matter.","w":"A wastes context on unambiguous cases; C is false - examples complement criteria, not replace them; D is false - code review is a prime few-shot use case.","x":["Incorrect. Twenty or more examples bloat the context and dilute the examples that matter.","Correct. Two to four examples aimed at the genuinely ambiguous cases, showing the reasoning and the exact output format, are most effective.","Incorrect. Examples complement written criteria and do not replace them.","Incorrect. Code review is a good use of few-shot examples."]},
{"id":"Q10","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A customer's opening message is: \"I want to speak to a human agent immediately. Do not try to help me yourself.\" Your agent has access to the customer's account and can see that the issue appears to be a straightforward product replacement - a case the agent resolves correctly 95% of the time. The engineering team suggests the agent should attempt resolution first since it would be faster for the customer. What should the agent do?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Attempt resolution first, since the issue is simple - escalate only if the automated attempt fails or the customer asks again"},{"l":"B","t":"Run sentiment analysis to quantify the customer's frustration level and escalate only if it exceeds a configurable threshold"},{"l":"C","t":"Escalate immediately: an explicit request for a human is honored at once, without first attempting the product replacement"},{"l":"D","t":"Acknowledge the request, then ask a few clarifying questions to see whether the customer would accept automated help after all"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Explicit customer demands for a human agent are a first-class escalation trigger, honored immediately and unconditionally. Overriding a clearly stated preference to demonstrate capability damages trust regardless of how resolvable the issue appears.","w":"A and D do exactly what the customer explicitly prohibited; B substitutes a computed proxy for a signal that couldn't be clearer.","x":["Incorrect. Trying to resolve it first does exactly what the customer asked the agent not to do.","Incorrect. A computed sentiment score replaces a signal that could not be clearer.","Correct. An explicit request for a human is honoured at once, without first attempting the replacement.","Incorrect. Asking clarifying questions to see if the customer would accept automated help also ignores the request."]},
{"id":"Q11","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Your team proposes automatically escalating support cases to human agents whenever the agent's sentiment analysis detects negative customer sentiment above a configurable threshold. The rationale is that frustrated customers deserve human attention. During a pilot, you notice a calm, polite customer with a complex policy exception being handled autonomously (sentiment score: 2/10), while an annoyed customer asking to reset a password gets escalated (sentiment score: 8/10). What is the fundamental flaw in this approach?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The sentiment model was trained on general text and needs retraining on support conversations before its scores can be trusted"},{"l":"B","t":"Sentiment doesn't track complexity: annoyed customers with simple issues get escalated, calm ones needing policy exceptions do not"},{"l":"C","t":"The threshold is set too low; raising it from 8/10 to 9/10 would cut the false positives seen in the pilot without losing real cases"},{"l":"D","t":"Sentiment should be scored once at the end of the conversation, when the customer's overall mood is clear, rather than turn by turn"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Sentiment measures how the customer feels, not whether the case genuinely requires human intervention. The correlation between frustration and case complexity is weak - escalation criteria should be based on case characteristics (policy gaps, authorization requirements, inability to progress) rather than emotional signals.","w":"A improves the proxy without fixing the fundamental disconnect; C tunes a flawed metric; D changes timing but not the underlying mismatch.","x":["Incorrect. Retraining the sentiment model improves a proxy but does not fix the mismatch with case complexity.","Correct. Sentiment does not track complexity. Annoyed customers with simple issues get escalated, and calm ones needing policy exceptions do not.","Incorrect. Changing the threshold tunes a flawed metric and does not fix the basis for escalating.","Incorrect. Scoring once at the end changes the timing but not the mismatch."]},
{"id":"Q12","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"In long multi-issue support sessions, your agent's later responses increasingly misquote refund amounts, confuse order numbers between issues, and state incorrect dates. Investigation reveals two compounding problems: (1) earlier transactional details are being condensed into vague summaries like \"the customer's refund\" during progressive summarization, and (2) each `lookup_order` call dumps 40+ fields into context when only 5 are relevant to the current issue. Which TWO changes address these root causes?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep amounts, dates, order numbers and statuses in a persistent case-facts block included in every prompt, outside the summarized history"},{"l":"B","t":"Raise `max_tokens` so the agent has room to restate amounts, dates and order numbers in full in each of its responses"},{"l":"C","t":"Trim each tool result to only the fields relevant to the current issue before it enters the conversation context"},{"l":"D","t":"Have the agent ask the customer to reconfirm key order numbers and refund amounts every few turns, so any drift is caught early in the session"}],"a":["A","C"],"r":"The paired fix addresses both root causes: (1) persisting transactional facts in a dedicated block protects them from lossy summarization, and (2) trimming verbose tool outputs at the source prevents irrelevant fields from consuming context budget and creating confusion.","w":"B controls output length, not context quality; D outsources a system memory problem to the customer.","x":["Correct. A persistent case-facts block outside the summarised history protects transactional details from lossy summarisation.","Incorrect. A larger max_tokens controls output length, not the quality of what is held in context.","Correct. Trimming each tool result to the relevant fields stops irrelevant data using context budget and causing confusion.","Incorrect. Asking the customer to reconfirm details pushes a system memory problem onto the customer."]},
{"id":"Q13","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"The `get_customer` tool returns three matching records for a customer named \"Sarah Johnson.\" The agent selects the record in the same city as the support phone number's area code and proceeds to process a refund. The selected account turns out to be a different Sarah Johnson. What should the agent do when tool results return multiple matches?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Pick the record with the most recent order activity, since that customer is the one most likely to be contacting support today"},{"l":"B","t":"Use the first record returned, because the lookup service ranks matching customers by relevance to the query"},{"l":"C","t":"Ask the customer for an extra identifier, such as account number, email or phone, and confirm the right record before acting"},{"l":"D","t":"Escalate to a human agent, because choosing between duplicate customer records is an identity decision automation should not make"}],"a":["C"],"r":"When multiple records match, the agent should request additional identifiers for disambiguation. Heuristic selection by city, recency, or position is unreliable and risks acting on the wrong account - a particularly dangerous failure when financial operations follow.","w":"A and B use unreliable heuristics; D escalates unnecessarily when a simple clarification question can resolve the ambiguity.","x":["Incorrect. Picking the record with the most recent activity is an unreliable heuristic that can pick the wrong person.","Incorrect. The first record returned is not the right person just because of its position in the list.","Correct. Ask the customer for another identifier such as account number, email or phone, and confirm the right record before acting, especially before a financial operation.","Incorrect. Escalating is unnecessary when a simple clarifying question can resolve the ambiguity."]},
{"id":"Q28","s":2,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Mid-way through an extended codebase exploration session, you notice Claude's responses are becoming vague and generic. It references \"typical patterns\" and \"standard approaches\" instead of the specific class names and method signatures it discovered earlier. Response quality is visibly degrading, but you're not ready to end the session - you're mid-investigation. Which built-in command helps immediately?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"`/memory`, which shows which configuration files are loaded and helps diagnose inconsistencies"},{"l":"B","t":"`/fork`, which branches the session so the exploration can continue in a fresh parallel copy"},{"l":"C","t":"`/compact`, which compresses the conversation history so the session can continue with less context"},{"l":"D","t":"`/rules`, which reloads the `.claude/rules/` files and resets how the session is behaving"}],"a":["C"],"r":"/compact is the in-session relief valve for context saturation. It compresses accumulated conversation history - typically bloated with verbose tool outputs and discovery - allowing the session to continue with restored response quality.","w":"A is diagnostic not therapeutic; B is for branching not compression; D addresses rule loading not context bloat.","x":["Incorrect. /memory is diagnostic and does not relieve context saturation.","Incorrect. /fork branches the session and does not compress its history.","Correct. /compact compresses the conversation history so the session continues with restored response quality.","Incorrect. /rules does not exist as a way to reset context bloat, and the problem is not rule loading."]},
{"id":"Q37","s":3,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Final research reports from your system state findings as facts but cite no sources. Investigation reveals the problem is structural: each summarization step compresses the web search agent's source attributions away. By the time findings reach the synthesis agent, they're anonymous claims like \"the market is growing at 15%\" with no indication of where that figure came from. What is the fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Have the report agent add citations at the end, matched to the claims by topic so every finding appears sourced"},{"l":"B","t":"Have the synthesis agent note that sources are available on request, keeping the report short while still signalling its rigor"},{"l":"C","t":"Have every subagent output claim-source pairs (finding, URL or document, excerpt) that downstream agents preserve and merge"},{"l":"D","t":"Cut the pipeline to a single summarization step, so there are fewer places where attributions can be dropped"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Provenance survives only when it's structural: claim-source mappings travel as first-class data through every agent hop. The synthesis agent merges attributed claims rather than compressing anonymous assertions. Attribution is preserved by design, never reconstructed after the fact.","w":"A fabricates citations - worse than none; B advertises attribution the system no longer has; D reduces but doesn't eliminate the stripping since even one summarization step can lose sources.","x":["Incorrect. Adding citations at the end matched by topic can attach sources to claims they do not support, which is fabricated provenance.","Incorrect. Saying sources are available on request advertises attribution that the system no longer has.","Correct. Have every subagent output claim-source pairs (finding, URL or document, excerpt) that downstream agents preserve and merge, so provenance is structural.","Incorrect. Even one summarisation step can drop sources, so cutting stages reduces the problem without removing it."]},
{"id":"Q38","s":3,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Two credible industry sources report different market size figures for the same sector: Source A says $12.4 billion (published January 2026), Source B says $8.7 billion (published March 2025). One subagent's summary simply used the larger figure. How should conflicting data from credible sources be handled?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Average the two figures to $10.55 billion so the report gives one balanced estimate that draws fairly on both credible sources"},{"l":"B","t":"Use the more recent figure and drop the older one, since market sizes change and the newer source supersedes it"},{"l":"C","t":"Report both figures with their sources, and require publication dates in structured outputs so time differences are visible"},{"l":"D","t":"Leave both figures out of the report, since presenting either one risks stating an inaccurate number as fact"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Conflicts are information, not errors. Both values with attribution let the coordinator make an informed reconciliation decision. Publication dates in structured output reveal whether the figures measure different time periods rather than contradicting each other.","w":"A invents a number no source reported; B automates a decision that requires judgment; D discards two credible data points.","x":["Incorrect. Averaging the two figures invents a number that no source reported.","Incorrect. Using only the newer figure automates a decision that needs judgement.","Correct. Report both figures with their sources, and require publication dates in structured outputs so time differences are visible.","Incorrect. Leaving both out discards two credible data points."]},
{"id":"Q39","s":3,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"In multi-agent synthesis, your final reports consistently omit or under-represent findings from the middle sections of long aggregated subagent outputs. The first and last subagent's findings are well-represented, but the middle subagent's critical financial data is often missing from the synthesis. What cognitive effect explains this, and how do you mitigate it?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"\"Lost in the middle\": models attend less to the middle of long inputs, so put key-findings summaries first and add section headers"},{"l":"B","t":"The middle subagent produces weaker output, and the synthesis agent is correctly giving its findings less weight in the report"},{"l":"C","t":"Raise `max_tokens` so the synthesis agent has enough output room to include the middle sections as well"},{"l":"D","t":"Randomize the order of subagent outputs on each run so that no single subagent's findings are consistently the ones that get left out"}],"a":["A"],"r":"The \"lost in the middle\" effect is well-documented: models attend more reliably to the beginning and end of long contexts. Placing key summaries at the start and using explicit section headers ensures critical findings receive appropriate attention regardless of position.","w":"B incorrectly blames output quality; C adds output space not input attention; D distributes the problem randomly rather than solving it.","x":["Correct. This is the 'lost in the middle' effect. Models attend less to the middle of long inputs, so put key-findings summaries first and add section headers.","Incorrect. The middle subagent's output is not weaker. Position is the cause, so the synthesis agent is not correctly down-weighting it.","Incorrect. A larger max_tokens adds output room and does not improve attention to the input.","Incorrect. Randomising the order spreads the problem around and does not solve it."]},
{"id":"Q41","s":3,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Your multi-agent system needs crash recovery capability. If the coordinator fails mid-research (due to a timeout, network issue, or process restart), the system currently loses all completed subagent work and must restart from scratch - wasting up to 10 minutes of prior computation. How should crash recovery be designed?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Re-run the whole pipeline from the beginning, which is simplest and guarantees the findings are consistent"},{"l":"B","t":"Cache every raw API response in a database and replay the responses in order when the coordinator restarts"},{"l":"C","t":"Have each subagent write its findings and status to a manifest; on restart the coordinator loads it and resumes unfinished work"},{"l":"D","t":"Rely on the coordinator's conversation history, which persists across process restarts and already records which steps have completed"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Structured state persistence via manifest files lets the coordinator resume intelligently. Each agent writes its findings and completion status; on restart, the coordinator reads the manifest, skips completed work, and picks up where it left off.","w":"A wastes all prior computation; B replays raw responses rather than resuming at the logical checkpoint; D assumes session persistence that doesn't survive process restarts.","x":["Incorrect. Re-running everything wastes all the prior computation.","Incorrect. Replaying raw API responses resumes at the wrong level instead of at the logical checkpoint.","Correct. Have each subagent write findings and status to a manifest. On restart the coordinator loads it, skips completed work and resumes the rest.","Incorrect. A conversation history does not necessarily survive a process restart, so it cannot be relied on to record progress."]},
{"id":"Q43","s":3,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A synthesis report needs to clearly distinguish between findings that are well-supported by multiple sources and topic areas where the research was incomplete due to failed searches or unavailable sources. Currently, the report presents everything with equal confidence. How should the output be structured?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Leave out any topic where sources were unavailable, so the published report contains only findings that are well-supported by evidence"},{"l":"B","t":"Add coverage annotations marking which findings are well-supported and which topics have gaps from missing sources"},{"l":"C","t":"Add a disclaimer at the end noting that some sources may not have been available during the research run"},{"l":"D","t":"Present all findings at the same confidence level so the report reads consistently and keeps its authority"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Coverage annotations let report consumers distinguish well-supported claims from areas where research was incomplete. This enables informed decision-making rather than false confidence in uniformly-presented but unevenly-supported findings.","w":"A silently drops important topics; C is too generic to be actionable; D is dishonest about the report's evidence basis.","x":["Incorrect. Leaving out topics with missing sources silently drops important topics.","Correct. Coverage annotations mark which findings are well supported and which topics have gaps from missing sources, so readers can judge the confidence.","Incorrect. A generic disclaimer at the end is not actionable and does not show where the gaps are.","Incorrect. Presenting everything at the same confidence is dishonest about the evidence behind the report."]},
{"id":"Q49","s":4,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"During a multi-hour codebase exploration session, your agent begins giving increasingly vague responses. Asked about the UserAuth class it discovered two hours ago, it describes \"typical authentication patterns\" instead of the specific methods, decorators, and middleware it found in the actual codebase. This context degradation is making the agent unreliable but the investigation isn't complete. Which TWO practices counteract this?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Switch to a model with a larger context window and keep accumulating the full conversation so earlier findings stay available"},{"l":"B","t":"Keep a scratchpad file of key findings (class names, method signatures, decisions) and have the agent consult it for later questions"},{"l":"C","t":"Ask the agent to try harder and be more specific in each follow-up, naming the exact class or method you need it to recall"},{"l":"D","t":"Delegate verbose investigations, such as tracing the auth middleware chain, to subagents that return distilled summaries"},{"l":"E","t":"Repeat the important questions word-for-word in each message so the model sees them more recently and gives them more weight than older turns"}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"Both practices manage what occupies the finite context: a scratchpad persists precise findings outside the degrading conversation history, and subagent delegation keeps verbose exploration output out of the main context entirely, returning only distilled summaries.","w":"A postpones the same degradation at higher cost; C adds text without removing noise; E consumes more context with repetition.","x":["Incorrect. A larger window with the full conversation only postpones the same degradation, at higher cost.","Correct. A scratchpad file of key findings, such as class names and method signatures, persists precise details outside the degrading conversation history.","Incorrect. Asking the agent to try harder adds text and does not remove the noise.","Correct. Delegating verbose investigations to subagents that return distilled summaries keeps the exploration output out of the main context.","Incorrect. Repeating questions word for word consumes more context without restoring what was lost."]},
{"id":"Q69","s":6,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Your extraction system reports 97% aggregate accuracy across all document types, and leadership proposes eliminating human review for high-confidence extractions to reduce costs. Before approving this, what validation must be performed on the aggregate metric?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Accept the 97% aggregate as sufficient, since it exceeds the 95% threshold the team agreed on before the pilot began"},{"l":"B","t":"Break accuracy down by document type and field; the aggregate may hide weak segments such as handwritten invoices or tax IDs"},{"l":"C","t":"Randomly sample 1% of all extractions for spot-checking, which gives a statistically fair estimate of the true error rate"},{"l":"D","t":"Review only the extractions the model flagged as low confidence, since that is where the remaining errors are most likely to be found"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Aggregate accuracy can mask significant variance - near-perfect performance on common document types can compensate for poor performance on rare types. Per-segment validation ensures the system performs consistently before human review is reduced for any segment.","w":"A trusts a potentially misleading metric; C isn't targeted at segments where errors concentrate; D misses errors the model is confidently wrong about.","x":["Incorrect. A 97% aggregate can hide weak segments, so meeting the threshold is not sufficient.","Correct. Break accuracy down by document type and field. The aggregate may hide weak segments such as handwritten invoices or tax IDs.","Incorrect. A random 1% spot-check is not targeted at the segments where errors concentrate.","Incorrect. Reviewing only low-confidence items misses errors the model is confidently wrong about."]},
{"id":"Q84","s":3,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Your multi-agent system produces reports that mix financial data, news summaries, and technical specifications. Currently, synthesis converts everything into uniform prose paragraphs, making financial tables hard to read and technical specs unclear. How should the synthesis output handle different content types?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Convert everything to bullet points, so every section of the report has the same consistent and scannable structure"},{"l":"B","t":"Use each content type's natural format: tables for financial data, prose for news, structured lists for technical findings"},{"l":"C","t":"Present all of the data as raw JSON so downstream tools and readers can process every section in exactly the same way afterwards"},{"l":"D","t":"Let the synthesis agent choose whatever format feels most natural for each section, based on how the content reads to it"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Different content types have natural presentation formats that preserve their readability and utility. Financial data reads best as tables, narrative content as prose, technical specs as structured lists. Forcing everything into one format loses the information structure.","w":"A loses table structure; C is for machines not readers; D is too vague to produce consistent results.","x":["Incorrect. Turning everything into bullet points loses table structure.","Correct. Use each content type's natural format: tables for financial data, prose for news and structured lists for technical findings.","Incorrect. Raw JSON is meant for machines and not for readers of a report.","Incorrect. Letting the agent pick whatever feels natural is too vague to give consistent results."]},
{"id":"Q85","s":3,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Subagents in your research system return verbose findings - full article excerpts, lengthy reasoning chains, and exploratory dead ends. By the time three subagents' outputs are aggregated, the synthesis agent's context is overwhelmed and it produces shallow summaries. What should change about how subagents structure their output?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Have subagents return their raw, unprocessed findings so the synthesis agent can decide for itself what matters"},{"l":"B","t":"Have subagents return structured data (key facts, citations, relevance scores) rather than verbose excerpts and reasoning"},{"l":"C","t":"Give the synthesis agent a larger context window so it can take in all three subagents' full outputs at once without truncation"},{"l":"D","t":"Cut the system to two subagents so the combined output stays small enough for the synthesis agent to process well"}],"a":["B"],"r":"When downstream agents have limited context budgets, upstream agents should return structured, distilled data rather than verbose content. Key facts, citations, and relevance scores communicate the essential findings without the reasoning chains and dead ends that consume context.","w":"A maximizes the problem; C postpones it at higher cost; D reduces research breadth to solve a formatting problem.","x":["Incorrect. Returning raw findings maximises the context problem.","Correct. Have subagents return structured data such as key facts, citations and relevance scores instead of verbose excerpts and reasoning.","Incorrect. A larger window postpones the problem at higher cost.","Incorrect. Cutting to two subagents reduces research breadth to solve a formatting problem."]},
{"id":"Q91","s":6,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Your extraction system reports 97% accuracy, and leadership wants to eliminate human review for high-confidence extractions. Before making this decision, you implement stratified random sampling of the 'high-confidence' population. The sampling reveals that while common invoice types are at 99% accuracy, handwritten invoices are at 62% and international invoices are at 71%. What does this demonstrate?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"That the 97% aggregate is accurate, since the weak segments are small enough that full automation remains safe overall"},{"l":"B","t":"That aggregate accuracy can hide very different segment performance; here it masked near-failure on two document types"},{"l":"C","t":"That handwritten and international invoices should be removed from the system entirely and processed by a separate manual team"},{"l":"D","t":"That the model must be retrained on handwritten and international documents before any automation decision is made"}],"a":["B"],"r":"This is exactly why segment-level validation must precede automation decisions: the 97% aggregate was hiding 62% accuracy on handwritten invoices. Automating 'high-confidence' extractions without segment validation would have silently produced incorrect results for specific document types.","w":"A ignores the variance the sampling revealed; C excludes documents instead of routing them to review; D jumps to retraining when routing to human review handles the immediate risk.","x":["Incorrect. The 97% aggregate hid weak segments, so full automation is not safe.","Correct. Aggregate accuracy can hide very different segment performance. Here it masked near-failure on handwritten and international invoices.","Incorrect. Removing those documents entirely is not the answer when they can be routed to review.","Incorrect. Retraining may help later, but routing to human review handles the immediate risk."]},
{"id":"Q92","s":6,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Your extraction system's human reviewers can only review 10% of all extractions due to capacity constraints. Currently, reviews are assigned by submission order (every 10th extraction). Error rates vary dramatically: low-confidence extractions have 15% error rates while high-confidence ones have 0.5%. How should review routing be redesigned?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep reviewing every 10th extraction, which gives an unbiased, representative sample of the system's overall quality over time"},{"l":"B","t":"Output field-level confidence, calibrate thresholds on a labeled validation set, and send low-confidence items to review"},{"l":"C","t":"Review only documents longer than 10 pages, since longer documents give the model far more chances to make an error"},{"l":"D","t":"Use the model's raw confidence scores as they are and route anything below 80% confidence to human review"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Calibrated confidence-based routing concentrates limited review capacity where errors actually occur. Field-level scores (not document-level) catch specific problematic fields, and calibration against labeled data ensures the thresholds are meaningful - unlike raw, uncalibrated confidence scores.","w":"A routes by submission order, not error risk; C uses page count as a proxy for error likelihood; D uses uncalibrated confidence that may not correlate with actual accuracy.","x":["Incorrect. Reviewing every tenth item spreads limited review capacity evenly instead of putting it where errors are most likely.","Correct. Output field-level confidence, calibrate thresholds on a labelled validation set, and send low-confidence items to review. This puts capacity where the errors are.","Incorrect. Page count is a weak proxy for error likelihood.","Incorrect. Raw confidence scores are not calibrated, so an 80% cut-off may not match actual accuracy."]},
{"id":"Q94","s":4,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"During a complex investigation spanning multiple sessions, your agent needs crash recovery capability. If the agent process dies mid-analysis (due to a network timeout or system restart), all findings from the current session are lost. How should you design for resilient state persistence?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Re-run the whole analysis from scratch after each crash, which keeps the results consistent and the design simple"},{"l":"B","t":"Have the agent periodically write findings and state to a manifest file; on restart, load it and resume unfinished work"},{"l":"C","t":"Rely on the conversation history, which persists across process restarts and lets the agent pick up where it stopped"},{"l":"D","t":"Keep all state in memory for speed, and accept that an occasional crash means repeating part of the investigation work"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Structured state persistence via manifest files makes the investigation resilient to crashes. The agent writes findings and status as it goes; after a restart, the manifest shows exactly what's done and what remains, enabling intelligent resumption rather than wasteful restart.","w":"A wastes all completed work; C assumes persistence that doesn't survive process death; D accepts avoidable data loss.","x":["Incorrect. Re-running the whole analysis after each crash wastes all completed work.","Correct. Have the agent periodically write findings and state to a manifest file. On restart, load it and resume the unfinished work.","Incorrect. Conversation history does not necessarily survive process death.","Incorrect. Keeping all state in memory accepts avoidable data loss."]},
{"id":"Q95","s":4,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"Your agent is exploring a large codebase and has accumulated extensive findings about the authentication module over the past hour. You notice its responses becoming increasingly vague - referencing 'the auth system' instead of the specific AuthMiddleware class and JWT validation methods it discovered earlier. The context window is filling with verbose Grep outputs. Which TWO practices help sustain coherent exploration?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Switch to a model with a larger context window and continue, so the earlier auth findings are not pushed out of context"},{"l":"B","t":"Keep a scratchpad file of key findings (class names, method signatures, decisions) that the agent consults for later questions"},{"l":"C","t":"Delegate verbose discovery, such as tracing the JWT validation flow, to subagents that return short summaries"},{"l":"D","t":"Ask the agent to be more specific in every response, naming the exact classes and methods it found instead of general summaries"},{"l":"E","t":"Repeat the original question in each message so the model sees it more recently and keeps its focus on the auth module"}],"a":["B","C"],"r":"The two practices manage what occupies finite context: a scratchpad persists precise findings outside the degrading conversation (AuthMiddleware, not 'the auth system'), and subagent delegation keeps verbose Grep results out of the main context entirely.","w":"A postpones the same degradation; D and E add text without removing noise.","x":["Incorrect. A larger context window only postpones the same degradation.","Correct. A scratchpad file of key findings, such as class names and method signatures, keeps precise details outside the degrading conversation.","Correct. Delegating verbose discovery to subagents that return short summaries keeps Grep results out of the main context.","Incorrect. Asking for specific wording adds text and does not remove the noise.","Incorrect. Repeating the question adds text and does not remove the noise."]},
{"id":"Q96","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Your support agent handles multi-issue sessions where customers raise 3-4 concerns in sequence. During these extended sessions, the agent needs to remember precise details - refund amount $127.43, order #889234, return window expires March 15 - from the first issue while working on the fourth. Currently, progressive summarization is condensing these into vague references like 'the customer's refund.' How do you preserve transactional precision?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Use a model with a larger context window so the conversation does not need to be summarized during long sessions"},{"l":"B","t":"Keep amounts, order numbers, dates and statuses in a persistent case-facts block in every prompt, outside the summary"},{"l":"C","t":"Ask the customer to restate the key details before each new issue, so the agent always has them fresh in context"},{"l":"D","t":"Turn off summarization and keep the full conversation history, so no detail from earlier issues can ever be condensed away"}],"a":["B"],"r":"A dedicated 'case facts' block persists precise transactional data outside the conversation that gets summarized. The summary can safely condense discussion flow while the case facts block preserves every dollar amount, order number, and date exactly as stated.","w":"A is expensive and doesn't prevent summarization; C outsources a system problem to the customer; D will eventually hit context limits on long sessions.","x":["Incorrect. A larger window does not stop summarisation from condensing details.","Correct. Keep amounts, order numbers, dates and statuses in a persistent case-facts block in every prompt, outside the summary, so they stay exact.","Incorrect. Asking the customer to restate details pushes a system problem onto the customer.","Incorrect. Keeping the full history will eventually hit context limits in long sessions."]},
{"id":"Q100","s":3,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Your web search subagent encounters a timeout while researching a complex topic. The subagent needs to report this failure to the coordinator for intelligent recovery. Which error propagation approach gives the coordinator the best information to decide what to do next?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Return an empty result set marked as successful - keeping the pipeline moving is more important than reporting a failure"},{"l":"B","t":"Stop the entire research workflow, since any subagent failure means the final report would be incomplete and misleading"},{"l":"C","t":"Return a generic 'search unavailable' status, so the coordinator handles every kind of failure through one simple path"},{"l":"D","t":"Return structured error context: the failure type, the query attempted, any partial results, and possible alternatives"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Structured error context gives the coordinator everything it needs for intelligent recovery: retry with a modified query, try an alternative source, proceed with partial results, or escalate. Generic statuses and silent suppression both hide the information recovery decisions require.","w":"A silently suppresses the failure, risking incomplete research presented as complete; B is disproportionate when partial recovery is possible; C hides the specific context the coordinator needs.","x":["Incorrect. An empty result marked successful silently suppresses the failure, so incomplete research looks complete.","Incorrect. Stopping the whole workflow is disproportionate when partial recovery is possible.","Incorrect. A generic 'search unavailable' status hides the context the coordinator needs.","Correct. Return structured error context: the failure type, the query attempted, any partial results and possible alternatives."]},
{"id":"Q114","s":5,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A reviewer wants to explore an alternative fix for a bug without losing the current session's progress, in case the alternative does not pan out. Which feature lets them branch off the current session state into an independent copy they can experiment in?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"`fork_session`, which creates an independent copy of the session that can diverge from the original"},{"l":"B","t":"End the current session and start over on the alternative fix, re-deriving the earlier findings only if they are needed"},{"l":"C","t":"Copy the project's `CLAUDE.md` into a new directory and start a second session there for the experiment"},{"l":"D","t":"Continue in the same session and ask Claude to set the current approach aside while it tries the alternative"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Forking a session creates an independent branch of the current state, letting the user experiment with an alternative approach while preserving the original session untouched if the experiment fails.","w":"B throws away the progress the reviewer wants to keep; C copies configuration, not conversation state; D keeps both attempts in one context, so the experiment is not independent of the original.","x":["Correct. fork_session creates an independent copy of the session that can diverge from the original, so the experiment does not affect the original.","Incorrect. Ending the session and starting over throws away the progress the reviewer wants to keep.","Incorrect. Copying CLAUDE.md copies configuration, not conversation state.","Incorrect. Staying in one session keeps both attempts in one context, so the experiment is not independent."]},
{"id":"Q137","s":4,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A developer asks their Claude Code agent to \"clean up this module,\" which is intentionally vague. The agent proceeds to rename public functions, change the module's external API, and reorganize file structure, none of which the developer expected. What does this scenario illustrate the need for?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Restricting the agent from renaming public functions in every future task, so external APIs can no longer change"},{"l":"B","t":"Trusting the agent's interpretation, since a broad cleanup is a reasonable reading of an intentionally vague request"},{"l":"C","t":"Giving the agent less context about the module, so it makes fewer assumptions about what the cleanup should cover"},{"l":"D","t":"Resolving ambiguity before acting, by asking a clarifying question or proposing a scoped plan before broad changes"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Vague instructions carry real risk of unintended scope; asking a clarifying question or proposing a plan for review before broad changes reduces the chance of surprising, disruptive edits like unplanned public API changes.","w":"C increases rather than decreases the risk illustrated; A is an overly broad, permanent restriction for what is really a process gap; B ignores the actual lesson from the scenario.","x":["Incorrect. Permanently banning renames is an overly broad restriction for what is really a process gap.","Incorrect. Trusting the agent's interpretation ignores the lesson that vague requests lead to unexpected scope.","Incorrect. Giving the agent less context increases the risk of assumptions.","Correct. Resolve the ambiguity before acting, by asking a clarifying question or proposing a scoped plan before broad changes."]},
{"id":"Q141","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A support agent harness logs every `stop_reason` value it receives over a week and finds a small number of responses with `stop_reason` \"`stop_sequence`\". What does this value indicate, and how should the harness treat it?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A tool call is pending and must be executed immediately, just as when the response stops with `tool_use`"},{"l":"B","t":"It should not appear in normal operation, so those responses point to corrupted logs or a misbehaving client library"},{"l":"C","t":"It indicates a system-level error, so the harness should end the session and report the failure to the user at once"},{"l":"D","t":"Claude emitted a configured stop sequence; treat it like a normal end of turn unless that sequence needs special handling"}],"a":["D"],"r":"A `stop_sequence` stop reason means generation halted because a configured stop string was produced; absent any special handling tied to that specific sequence, the harness treats it as a completed turn rather than a pending tool call or error.","w":"B and C mischaracterize a normal, expected stop reason as an error or impossibility; A confuses it with the distinct `tool_use` stop reason.","x":["Incorrect. A pending tool call is signalled by tool_use, not stop_sequence.","Incorrect. stop_sequence is a normal, expected stop reason, not a sign of corruption.","Incorrect. It is not a system-level error, so ending the session is wrong.","Correct. Claude produced a configured stop sequence. Treat it like a normal end of turn unless that sequence needs special handling."]},
{"id":"Q142","s":3,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A coordinator in a research pipeline wants to limit how many subagents can be active simultaneously to control cost, even though more could theoretically run in parallel. What is a reasonable way to balance latency against cost here?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Run one subagent at a time, since sequential dispatch keeps cost fully predictable and easy to attribute per task"},{"l":"B","t":"Run every subagent in parallel and absorb the cost, since faster research justifies the higher spend on most queries the team runs"},{"l":"C","t":"Set no limit and review spending monthly, since latency matters more to users than the cost of each query"},{"l":"D","t":"Impose a concurrency cap: dispatch up to the cap at once and queue the rest, trading some latency for predictable cost"}],"a":["D"],"r":"A concurrency cap lets the system still benefit from parallelism while keeping peak cost and resource usage predictable, which is a reasonable middle ground between maximum parallelism and strictly serial execution.","w":"B ignores the stated cost concern entirely; A sacrifices all latency benefits of parallel subagents; C dismisses a legitimate constraint the scenario explicitly raises.","x":["Incorrect. Running one subagent at a time gives up all the latency benefit of parallelism.","Incorrect. Running everything in parallel ignores the stated cost concern.","Incorrect. No limit with a monthly review dismisses a legitimate constraint.","Correct. Impose a concurrency cap. Dispatch up to the cap at once and queue the rest, trading some latency for predictable cost."]},
{"id":"Q144","s":2,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A `CLAUDE.md`-driven refactor workflow defines a multi-step process: analyze, propose plan, get approval, apply changes, run tests. A hook needs to prevent the \"apply changes\" step from running before the \"get approval\" step has recorded explicit user approval. What kind of enforcement best fits here?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A hook that blocks the apply step's write and edit tools until an approval flag is recorded in session state"},{"l":"B","t":"The written workflow steps in `CLAUDE.md`, which the agent reads at the start and follows in order throughout the session"},{"l":"C","t":"Applying the changes first and requesting approval afterwards, so reviewers can judge the real diff instead of a plan"},{"l":"D","t":"Dropping the approval step, since the tests that run afterwards already catch any change that should not have been made"}],"a":["A"],"r":"A hook that checks a recorded approval state before permitting the apply-step tools to run enforces the sequencing constraint deterministically, rather than depending on the agent following the documented order on its own.","w":"B relies on instruction-following rather than enforcement; C inverts the intended sequence entirely; D removes a safeguard the workflow was specifically designed to include.","x":["Correct. A hook that blocks the apply step's write and edit tools until an approval flag is recorded enforces the order deterministically.","Incorrect. Written steps in CLAUDE.md depend on the agent following them and do not enforce anything.","Incorrect. Applying changes first and asking for approval later inverts the intended sequence.","Incorrect. Dropping the approval step removes a safeguard the workflow was designed to have."]},
{"id":"Q146","s":6,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"In your extraction pipeline, a scope-partitioning design assigns different subagents to different sections of a long contract (parties and definitions, payment terms, termination clauses). What is the main benefit of this partitioning compared to one subagent reading the entire contract?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Partitioning suits contracts specifically, because their sections are standardized in a way other documents are not"},{"l":"B","t":"Partitioning returns results faster on any document length, since the sections are processed in parallel at no extra cost"},{"l":"C","t":"Partitioning removes the need for schema validation afterwards, since each subagent checks its own section as it goes"},{"l":"D","t":"Each subagent's context stays focused on one section, improving accuracy and keeping details from being diluted"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Scope partitioning keeps each subagent's attention on a bounded, relevant portion of a long document, which reduces the risk that details get lost or diluted when a single agent must attend to the entire document at once.","w":"B overclaims a universal speed benefit without regard to coordination overhead; C is false since validation remains necessary regardless of extraction strategy; A is an unjustified restriction to one document type.","x":["Incorrect. Partitioning is not limited to contracts because of standardised sections.","Incorrect. Parallel processing does not return results faster on any document length at no extra cost, because coordination adds overhead.","Incorrect. Validation is still needed after extraction, whatever strategy is used.","Correct. Each subagent's context stays focused on one section, which improves accuracy and keeps details from being diluted."]},
{"id":"Q150","s":2,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A code-review agent's prompt chain is fixed: lint check, then type check, then test run, then summary. A new requirement arrives: if the lint check fails badly enough, skip type checking and testing entirely and go straight to summary with a \"build blocked\" status. How should the chain be modified?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Add a conditional branch after lint: above a severity threshold, jump to the summary; otherwise continue the chain"},{"l":"B","t":"Skip type checking and testing on every run, since the summary step can report the lint results on its own anyway"},{"l":"C","t":"Keep the chain rigid and run every step regardless of outcome, then report the blocked build in the final summary"},{"l":"D","t":"Remove the lint step from the chain, so a bad lint result can no longer leave the build in a blocked state"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Introducing a conditional branch keyed on the lint step's actual outcome lets the chain short-circuit appropriately only when warranted, which matches the new requirement without discarding the chain's normal behavior for passing or minor-failure cases.","w":"C ignores the new requirement entirely by always running every step; D removes a needed check rather than handling its outcome; B incorrectly applies the short-circuit unconditionally to every run.","x":["Correct. Add a conditional branch after lint. Above a severity threshold jump to the summary, otherwise continue the chain.","Incorrect. Skipping type checking and testing on every run applies the short-circuit unconditionally.","Incorrect. Keeping the chain rigid ignores the new requirement to skip steps.","Incorrect. Removing the lint step discards a needed check and does not handle its result."]},
{"id":"Q155","s":4,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A developer asks their Claude Code agent to \"make the tests pass.\" The agent could either analyze why tests are failing and fix the underlying code, or modify the tests themselves to accept the current (possibly incorrect) behavior. Which orchestration-level safeguard best reduces the risk of the latter, undesirable outcome?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Give the agent the same access to test and implementation files, so it can choose whichever fix is quickest"},{"l":"B","t":"Trust the agent to fix the implementation, since making tests pass by editing them is rarely what it chooses to do"},{"l":"C","t":"A hook or verified rule that treats edits to test files as higher risk, requiring justification or approval first"},{"l":"D","t":"Stop the agent from running the test suite, so it cannot work towards a passing result by any route at all"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Treating test-file modification as a distinct, higher-scrutiny action reduces the risk of the agent taking the easy but wrong path of weakening tests to make them pass, which is a known failure mode worth explicitly guarding against.","w":"A provides no structural disincentive against the undesirable path; D removes the agent's usefulness entirely; B relies on hope rather than a safeguard given a documented risk.","x":["Incorrect. Giving the agent equal access to test and implementation files offers no protection against editing tests.","Incorrect. Trusting the agent relies on hope when the risk is documented.","Correct. A hook or rule that treats edits to test files as higher risk, requiring justification or approval first, guards against weakening tests.","Incorrect. Stopping the agent from running tests removes its usefulness."]},
{"id":"Q167","s":2,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A refactor agent is given both a \"`search_code`\" tool and a \"`run_tests`\" tool, but for a specific narrow task (fixing one typo in a comment) you want to force Claude to go straight to the edit without searching first, since the location is already known. How should `tool_choice` be configured for this one call?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Force the edit tool with `tool_choice`, since the location is already known and no search decision is needed"},{"l":"B","t":"Remove all tools from this request so Claude answers with the corrected comment text for you to apply"},{"l":"C","t":"Set `tool_choice` to \"none\" so Claude describes the edit in text, which is safer for a change this small"},{"l":"D","t":"Leave `tool_choice` at \"auto\" and state in the prompt that the location is known, so Claude skips the search"}],"a":["A"],"r":"When the correct next action is already known with certainty, forcing the specific tool via `tool_choice` removes an unnecessary decision point and guarantees the desired action, rather than leaving it to the model's judgment.","w":"B and C leave Claude unable to make the edit itself; D leaves the outcome to the model when a deterministic, forced choice is available and appropriate.","x":["Correct. When the location is already known, force the edit tool with tool_choice, which removes an unnecessary decision.","Incorrect. Removing all tools leaves Claude unable to make the edit itself.","Incorrect. tool_choice 'none' only makes Claude describe the edit in text, so it cannot make it.","Incorrect. Leaving tool_choice at 'auto' leaves the outcome to the model when a forced choice is available."]},
{"id":"Q178","s":4,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A developer-productivity agent needs to make a small, surgical change to one line deep inside a 2,000-line file. Why is the Edit tool generally preferred over using Write to overwrite the entire file with a regenerated version?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Write is limited to files under a certain size, so a 2,000-line file must be changed with Edit instead"},{"l":"B","t":"Edit runs faster than Write on large files, since it sends only the changed lines rather than the whole 2,000-line file"},{"l":"C","t":"Edit changes only the matched text, avoiding the risk of altering or dropping unrelated parts that a full rewrite carries"},{"l":"D","t":"There is little difference: both tools leave unrelated lines intact as long as the change itself is correct"}],"a":["C"],"r":"A surgical, matched edit changes only the intended content, while regenerating an entire large file from scratch via Write risks introducing unintended differences anywhere else in the file that was not meant to change.","w":"A is a false restriction that does not exist; B states a performance guarantee the platform does not make; D ignores the real correctness risk difference between the two approaches for this scenario.","x":["Incorrect. Write is not limited to small files, so this restriction does not exist.","Incorrect. The platform does not promise Edit is faster, so this is not the reason.","Correct. Edit changes only the matched text, avoiding the risk of altering or dropping unrelated parts that a full rewrite carries.","Incorrect. There is a real difference, since regenerating the whole file can change unrelated lines."]},
{"id":"Q183","s":2,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A code-generation tool's schema allows a \"language\" parameter as a free-text string, which has produced inconsistent values like \"python\", \"Python3\", and \"py\" across different calls. What schema change would most improve consistency?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Constrain \"language\" to an enum of known values, so the model picks from a fixed set instead of inventing variants"},{"l":"B","t":"Accept any string, including empty values, and normalize the variants downstream after the code has been generated and saved"},{"l":"C","t":"Keep it as free text but add a note in the description asking Claude to use consistent lowercase names"},{"l":"D","t":"Remove the language parameter, so the generator infers the language from the target file's extension every time"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Constraining a parameter to a fixed enum directly addresses inconsistent free-text values by giving the model a bounded, predictable set of choices rather than relying on unconstrained text generation to happen to be consistent.","w":"C does not structurally prevent the inconsistency it is meant to fix; D removes needed functionality; B widens rather than narrows the space of inconsistent possible values.","x":["Correct. Constrain 'language' to an enum of known values, so the model picks from a fixed set and does not invent variants.","Incorrect. Accepting any string and normalising later widens the space of inconsistent values.","Incorrect. A note asking for lowercase names does not structurally prevent the inconsistency.","Incorrect. Removing the parameter removes needed functionality."]},
{"id":"Q190","s":4,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A developer-productivity agent's dependency-analysis tool was originally scoped to only the main agent, but a newly added Explore subagent also needs read-only access to the same dependency graph to answer architecture questions without modifying anything. How should tool distribution be updated?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep the tool exclusive to the main agent, and have the Explore subagent build its own dependency analysis instead"},{"l":"B","t":"Create a copy of the tool under a new name for the Explore subagent, so each agent's tool can evolve independently"},{"l":"C","t":"Grant the Explore subagent access to the same tool, since its need is legitimate and the tool is read-only"},{"l":"D","t":"Move the tool from the main agent to the Explore subagent, since architecture questions are now the Explore agent's job"}],"a":["C"],"r":"A read-only tool with a legitimate new consumer can simply be distributed to that additional subagent as well; there is no need to duplicate implementation or restrict access artificially when the tool's scope already fits the new need.","w":"A forces unnecessary duplicated engineering effort; D removes the tool from an agent that still needs it; B creates redundant, harder-to-maintain duplicate implementations for no real benefit.","x":["Incorrect. Keeping the tool exclusive forces the subagent to duplicate engineering effort.","Incorrect. A renamed copy creates a redundant implementation that is harder to maintain.","Correct. Grant the Explore subagent access to the same tool, since its need is legitimate and the tool is read-only.","Incorrect. Moving the tool away from the main agent removes it from an agent that still needs it."]},
{"id":"Q192","s":5,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A CI pipeline configures an MCP server for its security-scanning tool at the project scope, committed in `.mcp.json`, so every pull request reviewed by any CI runner uses the same tool configuration. What is the main benefit of project-scoped (versus user-scoped) MCP configuration here?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Project scope is required before any MCP tool can be called from a CI runner, whatever else is configured"},{"l":"B","t":"Project-scoped servers start faster than user-scoped ones, which noticeably shortens the time each CI review takes to run"},{"l":"C","t":"There is no practical difference in CI, since runners have no user profile and treat both scopes identically"},{"l":"D","t":"Every CI runner and contributor gets the same version-controlled configuration, with no per-runner setup to drift"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Committing MCP configuration at the project scope ensures every consumer of the project (any runner, any contributor) uses the same, version-controlled setup, which is exactly the consistency benefit relevant to a shared CI pipeline.","w":"B is an unsupported performance claim; A is false since scope is about configuration sharing, not a precondition for any tool call; C ignores the real and relevant difference between the two scopes.","x":["Incorrect. Project scope is not required before an MCP tool can be called. It is about sharing configuration.","Incorrect. There is no claim that project-scoped servers start faster.","Incorrect. The two scopes differ in CI, because only project scope is shared through the repository.","Correct. Every CI runner and contributor gets the same version-controlled configuration, with no per-runner setup to drift."]},
{"id":"Q195","s":6,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"An extraction agent's MCP server for a third-party OCR service occasionally returns results in a different field-naming convention than the rest of the pipeline expects (camelCase instead of snake_case). Where should this inconsistency be resolved?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Normalize the field names in the MCP tool's result processing, so everything downstream sees one consistent convention"},{"l":"B","t":"Leave the names as they are, since Claude and the downstream code can match camelCase and snake_case fields reliably"},{"l":"C","t":"Have Claude convert the field names in its reasoning on each call, since it can recognize both conventions easily"},{"l":"D","t":"Switch the rest of the pipeline to camelCase, so it matches the OCR service and no conversion is needed anywhere at all"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Normalizing an external format inconsistency inside the tool's own processing layer produces one consistent contract for every consumer, which is more reliable than asking the model to manually translate conventions on every call.","w":"C pushes a mechanical transformation onto the model that is better handled deterministically; D disrupts consistency across the rest of the pipeline for the sake of one external quirk; B understates the real risk of downstream field-matching errors from inconsistent naming.","x":["Correct. Normalise the field names in the MCP tool's result processing, so everything downstream sees one convention.","Incorrect. Leaving the names mixed risks downstream field-matching errors.","Incorrect. Having Claude convert names on every call pushes a mechanical transformation onto the model.","Incorrect. Switching the whole pipeline to camelCase disrupts consistency for the sake of one external quirk."]},
{"id":"Q218","s":3,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A \"research-report\" skill bundles a formatting script, a citation-style guide, and specific instructions, intended to be invoked the same way whether the underlying project is about climate policy or software architecture. What property of skills makes this topic-independent reuse possible?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Its instructions and resources describe a reusable process, how to format and cite a report, not domain content"},{"l":"B","t":"Skills are tied to the topic they were written for, so reuse works only between projects on closely related subjects"},{"l":"C","t":"Skills need a topic-specific `CLAUDE.md` in each project first, which supplies the subject matter the skill works from"},{"l":"D","t":"Skills detect each project's topic automatically and adapt their instructions to it without any extra description"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Because a well-designed skill encodes a reusable process rather than domain-specific content, it can be invoked consistently across unrelated topics, exactly as described in the scenario, without needing per-topic modification.","w":"B and C contradict the reuse the scenario actually describes; D mischaracterizes what a skill does, since it provides a defined process rather than automatic topic awareness.","x":["Correct. A skill's instructions and resources describe a reusable process, how to format and cite a report, not domain content.","Incorrect. Skills are not tied to the topic they were written for.","Incorrect. A skill does not need a topic-specific CLAUDE.md before it can be used.","Incorrect. Skills do not detect a project's topic and adapt on their own. They provide a defined process."]},
{"id":"Q234","s":6,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"An extraction skill's bundled validation script was written assuming all dates are in ISO 8601 format, but a new batch of documents uses a regional date format the script does not recognize, causing silent misparses. Following good iterative development practice, what should happen once this gap is discovered?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Update the skill's script to handle the new format, and add a check that flags unrecognized formats instead of misparsing"},{"l":"B","t":"Document the unsupported format in the skill's README, so users know to watch for misparsed regional dates"},{"l":"C","t":"Retire the skill and build a new one from scratch, since its date handling rests on an assumption that has proved wrong"},{"l":"D","t":"Keep the script as it is and have users convert regional dates to ISO 8601 before submitting any documents for extraction"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Fixing the root-cause gap in the shared script, and adding an explicit check for unrecognized formats, directly addresses the discovered failure mode and prevents the same silent misparse from recurring on future documents.","w":"D pushes a fixable system limitation onto the user indefinitely; C is a disproportionate response to one addressable, fixable bug; B documents a known issue without actually resolving it.","x":["Correct. Update the skill's script to handle the new format, and add a check that flags unrecognised formats instead of misparsing them.","Incorrect. Documenting the limitation in a README does not fix the silent misparse.","Incorrect. Retiring the skill is disproportionate for one fixable bug.","Incorrect. Making users convert dates pushes a fixable limitation onto them indefinitely."]},
{"id":"Q247","s":4,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A boilerplate-generation agent's few-shot examples in its prompt were all written for Python projects. When asked to generate equivalent boilerplate for a Go project, results were noticeably weaker, closely mimicking Python idioms where they don't belong. What does this illustrate about few-shot examples?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Few-shot examples anchor output to the patterns shown, so mismatched examples can bias work in a different context"},{"l":"B","t":"Few-shot examples convey intent rather than syntax, so Python examples should transfer cleanly to the Go boilerplate"},{"l":"C","t":"Few-shot examples barely influence output, so the weaker Go results more likely reflect less Go in training data"},{"l":"D","t":"Few-shot examples mainly shape naming, so the Python bleed-through came from the instructions rather than examples"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Because few-shot examples strongly anchor output toward the patterns they demonstrate, examples drawn from a mismatched context can inappropriately bias generation for a different target context, which is exactly the Python-idiom bleed-through observed here.","w":"B and C both understate the real, demonstrated influence of few-shot examples; D arbitrarily restricts their influence to one narrow aspect when the scenario shows broader idiomatic bleed-through.","x":["Correct. Few-shot examples anchor output to the patterns shown, so mismatched examples can bias work in a different context.","Incorrect. Examples convey more than intent. They influence idioms and syntax too, as the Python bleed-through shows.","Incorrect. Few-shot examples do influence output strongly, so the weaker results are not simply down to less Go data.","Incorrect. Examples are not limited to shaping naming, and the bleed-through came from the Python examples."]},
{"id":"Q250","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A support agent's billing subagent returns dollar amounts in several inconsistent formats across different calls: \"$42.00\", \"42 USD\", and \"42.0\". Downstream code expects a single consistent numeric format. Where is the best place to resolve this inconsistency?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Instruct the model in the system prompt to always output amounts as plain decimals, without any currency symbols"},{"l":"B","t":"Reject any amount not already in the expected format, so only clean values ever reach the downstream code"},{"l":"C","t":"Have the downstream code accept every format it encounters, adding new parsing rules as new variants appear"},{"l":"D","t":"Normalize the format at the boundary, in the tool's result processing or a dedicated post-processing step"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Normalizing format inconsistency at a clear boundary, as close to the source of the variation as practical, produces one consistent contract for every downstream consumer, which is more reliable than asking the model to self-enforce formatting consistency.","w":"C pushes an ever-growing burden onto every downstream consumer indefinitely; A relies on instruction-following rather than a structural fix; B discards legitimate data over a formatting difference that is fixable.","x":["Incorrect. A system prompt instruction relies on instruction-following and is not a structural fix.","Incorrect. Rejecting amounts not in the expected format discards legitimate data over a fixable formatting difference.","Incorrect. Making downstream code accept every format adds an ever-growing burden to every consumer.","Correct. Normalise the format at the boundary, in the tool's result processing or a dedicated post-processing step."]},
{"id":"Q255","s":2,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A refactor agent's prompt gives three few-shot examples of correctly refactored functions, all of which happen to be fewer than ten lines long. When asked to refactor a 200-line function, results were notably weaker and less consistent with the demonstrated style. What is the most likely explanation?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Examples may not generalize to a very different scale or complexity, so a 200-line function falls outside their pattern"},{"l":"B","t":"Few-shot examples mainly shape formatting such as indentation, so the weaker results must come from somewhere else"},{"l":"C","t":"Long functions exceed what the model can refactor reliably in one pass, whatever examples it happens to be given in the prompt"},{"l":"D","t":"Function length has little effect on how examples generalize, so the examples' style itself must be inconsistent"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Few-shot examples anchor the model toward the specific pattern they demonstrate; examples that are all short and simple may not adequately represent the considerations needed for a much larger, more complex case, weakening generalization.","w":"D dismisses a real factor affecting how well examples generalize; C is an unsupported, overly absolute claim; B arbitrarily restricts the influence of examples to one narrow formatting aspect.","x":["Correct. Examples may not generalise to a very different scale or complexity, so a 200-line function falls outside their pattern.","Incorrect. Few-shot examples do not mainly shape formatting such as indentation.","Incorrect. Claiming long functions cannot be refactored reliably whatever the examples is an unsupported absolute.","Incorrect. Function length does affect how examples generalise, so dismissing it is wrong."]},
{"id":"Q256","s":6,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"An extraction pipeline needs to batch-process a backlog of 10,000 historical documents, with results needed by the next business day but not sooner, and at the lowest feasible cost per document. Which API mode and justification best fit this requirement?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The synchronous API with heavy parallelism, finishing the backlog in hours rather than waiting a full day for results"},{"l":"B","t":"Either API, since per-document cost is the same and only turnaround time differs between the two modes"},{"l":"C","t":"The synchronous Messages API, since batch jobs are limited to a few hundred documents and this backlog is far larger"},{"l":"D","t":"The Batch API, built for large, next-day workloads at a lower per-request cost than 10,000 synchronous calls"}],"a":["D"],"r":"A large backlog with a next-day (not immediate) deadline and a cost-sensitivity requirement is exactly the profile the Batch API is designed for, typically offering lower per-request cost than an equivalent volume of synchronous calls.","w":"C misstates batch capacity; A pays for speed the next-day deadline does not need; B ignores the real cost difference between the two modes.","x":["Incorrect. Heavy parallelism with the synchronous API pays for speed that a next-day deadline does not need.","Incorrect. Per-document cost is not the same, because the Batch API is cheaper per request.","Incorrect. Batch jobs are not limited to a few hundred documents.","Correct. The Batch API is built for large next-day workloads at a lower per-request cost than 10,000 synchronous calls."]}
];
const BS={"1":{"t":"Customer Support Resolution Agent","d":"Building a customer support resolution agent with the Claude Agent SDK handling returns, billing disputes, and account issues via custom MCP tools."},"2":{"t":"Code Generation with Claude Code","d":"Using Claude Code for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation with custom slash commands and CLAUDE.md configuration."},"3":{"t":"Multi-Agent Research System","d":"A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents for web search, document analysis, synthesis, and report generation to produce comprehensive cited reports."},"4":{"t":"Developer Productivity with Claude","d":"Building developer productivity tooling with the Claude Agent SDK for codebase exploration, legacy system understanding, boilerplate generation, and task automation."},"5":{"t":"Claude Code for Continuous Integration","d":"Integrating Claude Code into CI/CD for automated code review, test generation, and pull-request feedback with minimal false positives."},"6":{"t":"Structured Data Extraction","d":"Extracting information from unstructured documents, validating output against JSON schemas, handling edge cases, and integrating with downstream systems."}};
